  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "lesson-1",
      "name": "What is Intelligence?",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What does it mean to be intelligent?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore what intelligence means, examining the question from a human and machine perspective. They begin with their own intuitions and, through engaging with thought experiments and classroom discussions, they refine their thinking and emerge with a clearer understanding of their own definition of intelligence.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Logic-Based AI Unit Guide (one for each student)\n- Print and cut out copies of the Intelligence Card Sort activity (one for each pair of two students)",
        "student_overview": "**What does it mean to be intelligent?**\n\nExplore what intelligence means, examining the question from a human and machine perspective."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-1",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-2",
      "name": "AI’s Past, Present, and Future",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How did AI become what it is today?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore the history of AI and some of the major milestones and innovations in the story of its development. They also consider ways in which AI is still developing and brainstorm what it might look like in the future. By the end of the lesson, students are able to place AI in historical context and recognize how individuals have contributed to its progress.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the AI Innovations handout (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How did AI become what it is today?**\n\nExplore milestones in the history of AI and consider what AI might look like in the future."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-2",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-3",
      "name": "Algorithms",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**What makes a good algorithm?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn to formalize their intuitions about how to solve a problem by writing algorithms in the form of pseudocode. Students are presented with sentiment analysis as a fundamental problem an AI system might seek to solve and one that students are capable of brainstorming solutions for that could be written as a classical algorithm. Along the way, students explore the role of a market analyst—someone who uses sentiment analysis to understand how people feel about a topic.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Sentiment Analysis Activity Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**What makes a good algorithm?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn to how to perform sentiment analysis—figuring out the emotions conveyed by text—by writing an algorithm. Along the way, you'll explore the role of a market analyst: someone who uses sentiment analysis to understand how people feel about a topic."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-3",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-4",
      "name": "Hello, AI",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can code be used to store and use information?**\n\nIn this lesson, students develop programming skills with Python, exploring how to define variables that store information and that can be manipulated with operators. They also use functions to accept input and produce output. Through reading, writing, and debugging code, students identify ways that Python can be used to write programs that behave intelligently.",
        "student_overview": "**How can code be used to store and use information?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn to program with Python, exploring how to define variables that store information and that can be manipulated with operators. You'll explore how Python can be used to build programs that accept input and produce output."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-4",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-5",
      "name": "Defining Functions",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can writing reusable code be useful in designing programs?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn to write functions in Python that define intelligent behavior and that can be reused in their programs. Students learn the syntax for defining a new function in Python and explore how functions can accept inputs and produce outputs. In the process, students practice printing formatted strings and manipulating values with mathematical operators.",
        "student_overview": "**How can writing reusable code be useful in designing programs?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn to write functions in Python that define intelligent behavior and that can be reused in their programs. You'll learn the syntax for defining a new function in Python and explore how functions can accept inputs and produce outputs. "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-5",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-6",
      "name": "Optimization",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**Do the choices that are best in the moment always lead to the best outcome in the end?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn about optimization—finding the best solution to a problem—as one of the central goals of artificial intelligence and of operations researchers. Students take the perspective of an operations researcher by getting hands-on with a specific optimization technique called “hill climbing.” Through it, they encounter the broader idea of heuristics: methods for solving problems quickly, if only approximately. Implementing their own hill climbing algorithm to optimize a real-world scenario gives students the opportunity to learn about conditionals in Python.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Smart Thermostat Activity Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**Do the choices that are best in the moment always lead to the best outcome in the end?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn about optimization—finding the best solution to a problem—as one of the central goals of artificial intelligence and of operations researchers. You'll take the perspective of an operations researcher by getting hands-on with a specific optimization technique called “hill climbing.” Through it, you'll encounter the broader idea of heuristics: methods for solving problems quickly, if only approximately. As you implement your own hill climbing algorithm, you'll learn about conditionals in Python."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-6",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-7",
      "name": "Simulation (Part 1)",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What can I build more easily with loops?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn about simulations—simplified representations of reality—as tools for exploring, explaining, or predicting real-world events. Students build a simple simulation of a social media post going viral, using it to predict how the post will spread under different conditions. Implementing this simulation motivates students to learn about `for` loops in Python.",
        "preparation": "- Print out the Going Viral (Part 1) Activity Guide (one for each student)\n- Provide, or ask students to bring, calculators",
        "student_overview": "**What can I build more easily with loops?**\n\nLearn about simulations—simplified representations of reality—as tools for exploring, explaining, or predicting real-world events."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-7",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-8",
      "name": "Simulation (Part 2)",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**If simulations aren’t reality, why are they still useful?**\n\nStudents extend their exploration of simulations. They choose between simulating a social media post reaching a target number of views, a business reaching a target number of customers, or a colony of bacteria reaching a target size. Implementing such a simulation motivates students to learn about `while` loops in Python.",
        "student_overview": "**If simulations aren’t reality, why are they still useful?**\n\nExtend your exploration of simulations. You'll choose between simulating a social media post reaching a target number of views, a business reaching a target number of customers, or a colony of bacteria reaching a target size. As you implement such a simulation, you'll learn about `while` loops in Python."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-8",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-9",
      "name": "Debugging Intelligent Systems",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I find and fix errors in my code?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn strategies for finding and fixing bugs. Students start by looking at pseudocode and evaluating it for logical errors. They then get practice with software testing by looking at sample inputs and outputs for buggy programs and identifying the circumstances that cause programs to make mistakes. Students also get hands-on practice with debugging in Python, troubleshooting and fixing a variety of different errors that can arise in Python code.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Finding Bugs Activity Guide (one for each pair of students)",
        "student_overview": "**How can I find and fix errors in my code?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn strategies for finding and fixing bugs. You'll evaluate pseudocode for logical errors, get practice with software testing, and get hands-on practice with debugging in Python."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-9",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-10",
      "name": "Autocomplete",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How does a computer know what you meant to type?**\n\nIn this lesson, students investigate autocomplete as an everyday example of a rule-based AI system: to decide which words to suggest, autocomplete needs a rule to follow. Students look at one such rule—prefix matching—before implementing this rule in Python themselves. Along the way, students are introduced to objects and the methods that act on them, using Python's string methods to refine their solution.",
        "student_overview": "**How does a computer know what you meant to type?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll investigate *autocomplete* as an everyday example of a rule-based AI system: to decide which words to suggest, autocomplete needs a rule to follow. You'll learn one such rule—prefix matching—before implementing this rule in Python yourself. Along the way, you'll learn about *objects* and the *methods* that act on them."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-10",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-11",
      "name": "Decision Trees",
      "absolute_position": 11,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can AI use questions to make decisions?**\n\nIn this lesson, students meet decision trees—simple, branching structures that help computers make intelligent decisions. Students explore the kinds of problems decision trees solve, trace through example decision trees to see how they work, and finally build their own in Python, putting conditionals to work along the way.",
        "student_overview": "**How can AI use questions to make decisions?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll meet decision trees—simple, branching structures that help computers make intelligent decisions. You'll explore the kinds of problems decision trees solve, trace through example decision trees to see how they work, and finally build your own in Python, putting conditionals to work along the way."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-11",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-12",
      "name": "Limits of Logic-Based AI",
      "absolute_position": 12,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What are the limits of logic-based AI systems?**\n\nIn this unplugged lesson, students reflect on Unit 1 by exploring the limits of logic-based AI. As a class, they encounter three kinds of problems, each of which poses a challenge to logic-based AI systems: problems that rely on \"tacit knowledge\"—what you know but can't articulate; problems that cause \"combinatorial explosion\"—too many possibilities to cover with rules; and problems with unexpected inputs. Having seen these limits firsthand, students emerge motivated to explore machine learning as a solution in the next unit, following their end-of-unit project.",
        "student_overview": "**What are the limits of logic-based AI systems?**\n\nReflect on Unit 1 by exploring the limits of logic-based AI."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-12",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-13",
      "name": "Conversational AI Project",
      "absolute_position": 13,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "In this project, students implement a rule-based conversational AI program that asks questions of a user to make a decision. They brainstorm possible scenarios where conversational AI can be helpful and they use conditional logic to plan how their program will respond to user inputs. Then, students implement their conversational logic in Python and test their program’s ability to help people make decisions, evaluating the strengths and limits of their design.",
        "purpose": "- Print copies of the Conversational AI Project Planning Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "In this project, you'll implement a rule-based conversational AI program that asks questions of a user to make a decision. You'll implement your conversational logic in Python and test your program’s ability to help people make decisions."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-13",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-14",
      "name": "End of Unit Assessment",
