  ],
  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "d5b66cea-231a-4e6d-b656-9bb406d80bd9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "’Intelligence’ is one of those words we all use confidently until someone asks us to define it. Today, we’re going to pin down and test our own definitions.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d5b66cea-231a-4e6d-b656-9bb406d80bd9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e1b145ef-f47c-47cf-ae2d-3e2297cd3aec"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d5eef647-5d88-4c29-b311-addce6ad8af0",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *When you call a person—or an animal, or a computer—”intelligent,” what do you really mean?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prime students’ thinking about what ‘intelligence’ means. Students should surface their working definitions and notice they’re not all the same. Strong responses describe what the brain (or a system) is doing, not just its outcomes: for example, “learns from mistakes,” or “figures things out through trial and error.” Watch for outcome-oriented answers, like “gets good grades.” When you hear these, push students towards the underlying mechanism: “What is the brain doing that makes getting good grades possible?” If students struggle to describe what makes things intelligent, ask them for examples of objects that are not intelligent and compare: what makes some things intelligent and others not? To end the discussion, acknowledge that it’s okay to not agree on a single definition of intelligence. The point is simply to start to think about what ‘intelligence’ really means."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d5eef647-5d88-4c29-b311-addce6ad8af0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e1b145ef-f47c-47cf-ae2d-3e2297cd3aec"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e1307fb6-6dcb-46fa-9836-bb37eae473c7",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "It’s okay for us not to have a clear definition of ‘intelligence’ yet. For the next few minutes, I want you to write down your own definition based on what you shared or heard from other classmates.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e1307fb6-6dcb-46fa-9836-bb37eae473c7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e1b145ef-f47c-47cf-ae2d-3e2297cd3aec"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b49d7f98-ccea-4cbc-bc34-6e70d67d4215",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Logic-Based AI Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students 1–3 minutes to write down their working definition of intelligence in the Lesson 1 Warm Up section. Tell them they’ll come back to it at the end of class to refine it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b49d7f98-ccea-4cbc-bc34-6e70d67d4215",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e1b145ef-f47c-47cf-ae2d-3e2297cd3aec"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b5ac0d5-7a79-4f99-aac2-7435a10e3b89",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You have a working definition of ‘intelligence.’ Now, let's stress-test it. I'm going to give each pair of you a deck of cards. Each card describes a behavior—sometimes from a person, sometimes from an animal, sometimes from a machine. Your job is to decide what kind of 'intelligence'—if any—that behavior represents, and to group cards that show the same kind of intelligence together. There's no single right answer. What matters is that your group can defend the way you sorted.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Intelligence Card Sort",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b5ac0d5-7a79-4f99-aac2-7435a10e3b89",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f2bdecd6-f9c9-4e79-ac7c-6daedd8d6926",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the Intelligence Card Sort instructions to students. Take questions before grouping.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students in pairs of two.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "It’s okay if, with an odd number of students, you have a group of three."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f2bdecd6-f9c9-4e79-ac7c-6daedd8d6926",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a5aa1e59-0ccd-4c27-bc5d-b6d080efec62",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student an Intelligence Card Sort set.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the Intelligence Card Sort instructions to students while they work.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate**: Listen for groups that lump everything into one category (e.g., “It’s all just following rules”) or that split everything into “human” vs. “machine” without considering finer distinctions. If groups stall, ask: “Is the way a calculator solves a problem the same kind of thinking as the way a dog learns a new trick?” or “What about the thermostat—is that intelligence at all?” Reassure pairs who want a right answer that the goal is to surface their reasoning. Disagreements between groups are exactly what we want.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How did you decide which cards belonged together?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface students’ thinking from the Intelligence Card Sort activity. Students should recognize that intelligence isn’t one thing: it could be rule-following, learning from experience, responding to the environment, or reasoning about new situations. A strong response might name a category and describe why certain cards are part of it: for example, “these cards all learn from the experience, like the baby learning to walk.” Watch out for vague or outcome-only groupings: for example, “these are all the smart cards.” Offer more food for thought: “It’s good you think these cards are smart, but what are they doing that’s smart?” End the discussion by highlighting 2–3 different ways that pairs sorted their cards.”"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a5aa1e59-0ccd-4c27-bc5d-b6d080efec62",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a73546c-c49b-43d8-b8d0-000885c91d15",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "What you’ve just done—having different ideas about what counts as intelligence—is exactly the problem people have been arguing about for decades, especially when it comes to computers. In 1950, a mathematician named Alan Turing proposed one way to settle these debates. Let’s see what you think of his answer.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a73546c-c49b-43d8-b8d0-000885c91d15",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "074b9278-4867-4dc4-b227-16b2c0ffdd48",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define the *Turing test*. Emphasize that it’s a behavioral test: it doesn’t care what’s happening inside the machine, only what comes out.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "The Turing Test in Action"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "074b9278-4867-4dc4-b227-16b2c0ffdd48",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "88588601-71f2-480b-aa2d-78faa6738181",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Alan Turing was a mathematician who proposed a simple idea: to test for intelligence, it didn’t matter what a machine was actually doing—it only mattered whether it could hold a conversation as well as a human.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "88588601-71f2-480b-aa2d-78faa6738181",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "af7600e3-7a03-48d4-ae2d-4017afaee5ce",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Turing’s goal was to make a reasonable assessment of artificial intelligence, or AI. Let’s define what we mean by AI before we move any further.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "af7600e3-7a03-48d4-ae2d-4017afaee5ce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ec2a392d-f4df-40d7-8c2b-07a24d64c614",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *artificial intelligence*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ec2a392d-f4df-40d7-8c2b-07a24d64c614",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fab11ce4-e892-47d7-9261-1054f9754a86",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, let’s see the Turing Test in action. In this test, a human interviewer talked to two musical artists—one of which was AI. Let’s see how the artists responded to three of the interviewer’s questions.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fab11ce4-e892-47d7-9261-1054f9754a86",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aa294095-fce8-483c-89a7-3982ac7f60b3",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to raise their hand if they think A is the AI. Ask students to raise their hand if they think B is the AI."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aa294095-fce8-483c-89a7-3982ac7f60b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "58d924f4-8a04-42f3-9abf-457c82b33a0c",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s the next question and the two responses. Remember, one of these responses was generated by AI.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "58d924f4-8a04-42f3-9abf-457c82b33a0c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fd76f544-623c-479c-a51f-cb47b37a90da",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to again raise their hand if they think A is the AI. Ask students to raise their hand if they think B is the AI."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fd76f544-623c-479c-a51f-cb47b37a90da",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "700c6fb6-02d2-48a5-bb35-9be2efb792ee",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s the next question and the two responses. Remember, one of these responses was generated by AI.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "700c6fb6-02d2-48a5-bb35-9be2efb792ee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b578db7a-7448-4554-bf59-dea822dff7d8",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to once again raise their hand if they think A is the AI. Ask students to raise their hand if they think B is the AI."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b578db7a-7448-4554-bf59-dea822dff7d8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5bbcf3ac-2395-4703-9274-57cd834f573a",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Are you ready for the reveal?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5bbcf3ac-2395-4703-9274-57cd834f573a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2bbdcaa9-227d-4428-8b4a-84276153cecc",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Artist A was actually Taylor Swift in an interview for the New York Times. Artist B wasn’t real—instead, their responses were generated entirely by AI. If you thought Artist B was real, it passed the Turing test. So, what do you think? Is the Turing test an accurate test of intelligence? For the next 10 minutes, you’ll gather your thoughts and debate them with AI.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Optionally introduce students to the comic at https://imgs.xkcd.com/comics/turing_test.png, which riffs on the fundamental idea behind the Turing test."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2bbdcaa9-227d-4428-8b4a-84276153cecc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fbafe8ab-8d67-4d22-9e30-9921dc3c5d55",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Debating Intelligence",
        "progression_name": "Debating Intelligence"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fbafe8ab-8d67-4d22-9e30-9921dc3c5d55",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2df5cb6a-6041-4902-9677-cc4dd2e9349e",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2df5cb6a-6041-4902-9677-cc4dd2e9349e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e6bea07c-c12d-4f19-983d-5bcb6dcef435",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Levels 1–3. Students stay on the platform through the rest of the activity.\n- **Level 1:** Students write down their stance on the Turing test as a measure of intelligence.\n- **Level 2:** Students debate their stance with AI, using it to strengthen or adjust their argument.\n- **Level 3:** Students revisit and refine their stance based on the debate. This serves as the lesson's formative assessment.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ask students about their AI conversations on-screen. Look for:\n- Students who pick a side too quickly without engaging with counter arguments. You could ask: \"What's the best argument against your position? Has the AI tried it on you yet?\"\n- Students conflating \"fooling a human\" with \"thinking like a human.\" Ask students about whether these might be two different things..\n- Students who let the AI talk them out of their position without resistance. You could ask: \"Did the AI's argument actually mean something to you, or just sound persuasive?\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 3 responses to gauge understanding before the Wrap Up.\n- If most students offer a clear stance with at least one substantive reason, move to the wrap-up.\n- If responses lean on surface reasoning (e.g., \"it's just a computer,\" \"it's smart because it's fast\"), surface 2–3 student responses anonymously and ask the class to push on them: \"What would the Turing test say about this argument?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e6bea07c-c12d-4f19-983d-5bcb6dcef435",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7d86be1b-c70c-4fe6-9323-bd5d865a9311",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Whether or not you ended up convinced by Turing, you've now done what computer scientists have argued about for 75 years: what should count as intelligence in a machine. There's no settled answer. But the answer you would give shapes what we mean when we say a machine is 'smart.' Hold onto your stance—we'll use it again.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7d86be1b-c70c-4fe6-9323-bd5d865a9311",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7b7b3081-df64-41f3-9a00-54c277bcb4eb",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students back to their original definition of intelligence in the Unit Guide and have them refine it in light of today’s discussion. Give them about 90 seconds, then have 2–3 students share how their definition changed.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7b7b3081-df64-41f3-9a00-54c277bcb4eb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6b703fc-1e0f-48ed-ac0e-6f2abd5c810e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c2f243f7-917a-41ac-a9bd-ab90c43e8591",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c2f243f7-917a-41ac-a9bd-ab90c43e8591",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ded703ca-23ff-44c0-8a8f-2e63f0376ccf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ce333c25-a39c-4414-8d6f-856761533292",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Pair students up with a partner.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students talk with their partner about one of the examples of AI on the slide. Students should discuss what each example is capable of and what they think makes each example work.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As pairs discuss AI technologies with each other, listen for ways that students talk about intelligence.\n- If students describe a technology mostly in terms of output (e.g. “voice assistants answer questions”), consider prompting them to think about other intelligent parts of the process (e.g. How does the voice assistant know what words you said? How does it understand what your question means?)\n- To help students think about what inventions led to a technology, prompt students to think: “What does the computer have to figure out, step by step?” or “What had to exist before someone could build this?”\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What problems did people have to solve to create AI tools?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that AI is not a single idea or innovation, but involves solutions to many different problems. It’s okay if students don’t yet have a precise sense for those problems. If students struggle to identify problems, encourage them to brainstorm based on the different features of the AI tools: for example, a voice assistant needs to “listen to your voice” and “understand your question” and “speak an answer.”"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ce333c25-a39c-4414-8d6f-856761533292",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6e7f0096-f270-421a-bdba-088c9ca33cfe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "487cdf57-5012-4fe9-a475-0b0fb844b624",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, we’ll be exploring some of these ideas in AI to understand where they came from and what problems they solved.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "487cdf57-5012-4fe9-a475-0b0fb844b624",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6e7f0096-f270-421a-bdba-088c9ca33cfe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cb332a33-8790-4c60-8115-0813ff43c4d9",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cb332a33-8790-4c60-8115-0813ff43c4d9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6e7f0096-f270-421a-bdba-088c9ca33cfe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7b263f9-494d-4ece-8fb4-0e188f4dd375",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students explore different disciplines and technologies in AI’s history. The goal is to help students see that AI has come from work and research across many areas, to give students context for key milestones in AI’s development, and to give students the chance to consider how those milestones impacted today’s technology.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Pioneers and Ideas"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7b263f9-494d-4ece-8fb4-0e188f4dd375",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "535d7ff4-79eb-479c-b5fa-a9a6f2be43d2",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI isn’t just one idea or algorithm: it’s the result of many years of development and problem solving. The innovations that made AI possible came from research and ideas in multiple different fields.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "535d7ff4-79eb-479c-b5fa-a9a6f2be43d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e5f739e6-4818-4ace-a6b8-af6c6cde9024",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *interdisciplinary* and describe AI as the result of interdisciplinary research.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show some of the fields that have contributed to research in AI.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI Innovations Activity Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e5f739e6-4818-4ace-a6b8-af6c6cde9024",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "434527ec-5b6c-40e1-9b3c-e49c0b7c29bb",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "There are a lot of milestones in the history of AI. In this activity, you should pick one innovation related to AI that you’re excited about and learn more about it.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "434527ec-5b6c-40e1-9b3c-e49c0b7c29bb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d711d8fb-8a96-48ad-aed9-f755bc1990d0",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1 and 2.\n- **Level 1:** Students use AI Chat to explore different disciplines that have contributed to AI’s development and complete the “Exploring AI Milestones” page of their Activity Guide based on their interests.\n- **Level 2:** Students complete the formative assessment.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through Level 1, help students find a discipline and technology they’re interested in. Each discipline could lead to multiple technologies that students could explore: remind students that they don’t need to know about all of the technologies yet, they only need to pick one that looks interesting to them."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d711d8fb-8a96-48ad-aed9-f755bc1990d0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "92b30b86-0a39-44a9-b7f6-16433e7e4fe6",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Contributions to AI",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "- Level 1 is a choice level where students can choose a discipline of interest to them. If students finish early, they can explore another discipline to get a different perspective on other innovations that contributed to the development of AI. Or if students are excited about a different discipline or technology not covered in the level, you can encourage them to do their own independent research to complete the activity.\n- If time permits and if you have space in your classroom, students can draw or write about their chosen innovation in AI on a post-it note or on a sheet of paper and add it to an “AI Timeline” poster that can be hung in the classroom as a visual representation of the progression of AI."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Review student responses to the formative assessment to check that students can identify one or more ways that AI has developed in its history."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "92b30b86-0a39-44a9-b7f6-16433e7e4fe6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "44f93f8d-799c-4042-9672-0717713aead0",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "44f93f8d-799c-4042-9672-0717713aead0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ab14d672-c620-4b6f-bec0-bbf1a208fe90",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students consider predictions for the future of AI and make their own predictions. The goal is to help students see that today’s technology is not the end state for AI: technology continues to improve and develop, and students are encouraged to imagine what that might look like.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Future of AI"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ab14d672-c620-4b6f-bec0-bbf1a208fe90",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e67f4586-3310-4a54-b9b5-9b4269fd0007",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI is still being developed, and it might look very different a few years from now than it does today.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e67f4586-3310-4a54-b9b5-9b4269fd0007",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "74d65fc9-5147-4ef2-910f-eea42156d725",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students find a partner and direct students to the “Predict the Future” page of their Activity Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "74d65fc9-5147-4ef2-910f-eea42156d725",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9c5aef15-bcf5-44ba-ac7a-92e7f4da43b4",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Everyone has different ideas for what AI might look like in the future. Some of those predictions might come true soon, some might take a long time, and others might not happen at all.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9c5aef15-bcf5-44ba-ac7a-92e7f4da43b4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d6cd0c03-92ba-4a51-8b70-d7cca13f327d",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students complete the “Predict the Future” page of their Activity Guide.\n- Students will first read a set of predictions about the future of AI. In pairs, they should discuss what predictions they think are realistic or unrealistic. They should also talk about what predictions they are excited by.\n- Then, students make their own prediction for what they want to see in the future of AI.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As pairs fill in the activity guide, check on how students are thinking about the future and how they imagine it to be. When students are making their own prediction, this may be where students are most likely to provide vague answers like “scientists need to figure it out” or “we need better computers.” Help them get specific by drawing on the first activity: each step in AI’s history came from someone solving a particular problem in a particular discipline. What discipline could contribute to this innovation? What questions might people in that discipline need to ask?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "The questions in the “Predict the Future” section of the Activity Guide can be used to check student understanding of the lesson objectives. Check for whether students articulate their vision for a future of AI and what technical or societal advancements would make it possible."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d6cd0c03-92ba-4a51-8b70-d7cca13f327d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "879e0782-61b8-47ca-8107-4c0d72d0dd3e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students take out their Logic-Based AI Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** In their Unit Guide, students answer the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "879e0782-61b8-47ca-8107-4c0d72d0dd3e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "42e15a05-46ba-4120-acb6-06452cf97fc9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8960083b-066b-4f75-8d9d-f5b1828c7845",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8960083b-066b-4f75-8d9d-f5b1828c7845",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fcaae455-25a5-4be0-abb3-95d2a8ff4e56"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "92e390b2-3a12-4e48-abe0-1336d18d1354",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students learn about the task of sentiment analysis by playing a mini-game as a class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce What’s the Vibe?",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "What's the Vibe?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "92e390b2-3a12-4e48-abe0-1336d18d1354",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "558c6b08-46ca-446d-bee9-38256e45eb46",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s play a game called ‘What’s the Vibe?’! Here’s how the game works: I’ll show a sentence on the screen, and you’ll need to shout out, as quickly as you can, how you think the person who said the sentence is feeling. Ready?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "558c6b08-46ca-446d-bee9-38256e45eb46",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c0f0a87a-87b2-4b4f-9b9e-66be0d4e3e68",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show a sentence. Let students, all together, shout out how they think the person who said this sentence is feeling. Encourage quick, gut reactions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** As students shout out their guesses, repeat a few of their suggestions back to the class."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c0f0a87a-87b2-4b4f-9b9e-66be0d4e3e68",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e7c4e411-fa53-4721-8f18-bd4b4572c341",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Continue showing sentences and listening to students shout out their guesses.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How did you know how the person who said each sentence might be feeling?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to reflect on the idea of sentiment analysis before defining it formally. Emphasize how quickly students could tell how the person who said each sentence might be feeling: what knowledge did they rely on to arrive at that conclusion so quickly? Good responses might name specific skills: such as knowledge of emotionally charged vocabulary (e.g., “proud,” “awful,” “amazing”), or previous lived experience (e.g., “I’ve been to a disappointing movie before”). If students offer generic responses, such as “We just know!” or “The person told us how they feel!”, help them zero in on the specific skills or experiences they might have relied on. Once you’ve gathered ideas from 3–4 students, continue on."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e7c4e411-fa53-4721-8f18-bd4b4572c341",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "72c035e4-1b00-469c-81d3-0821f321c6cb",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In the world of artificial intelligence, there’s actually a word we can use to describe the challenge of figuring out how someone might be feeling based on the words they say. Let’s take a look.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "72c035e4-1b00-469c-81d3-0821f321c6cb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3518131d-a548-43a9-80e1-be47e9c30bfe",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _sentiment analysis_."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3518131d-a548-43a9-80e1-be47e9c30bfe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "086a7f26-7cae-4062-b3da-e074541531f7",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Just like you were doing a moment ago, sentiment analysis is about figuring out how people feel from text alone. It’s a common problem AI is being used to solve. Sentiment analysis can help businesses uses product reviews to guess how customers feel, help researchers uses social media to understand public reactions to major events, and even help doctors use patient notes to sense how patients are feeling—among many other uses!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "086a7f26-7cae-4062-b3da-e074541531f7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dee0f895-5826-4ba6-a076-bdbee50124c3",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, we’ll explore how we can tell computers how to solve problems like sentiment analysis.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dee0f895-5826-4ba6-a076-bdbee50124c3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ad38f9e4-c597-4481-8b14-a095ab7332be",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To tell a computer how to accomplish a task like sentiment analysis, we’ll create an algorithm.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ad38f9e4-c597-4481-8b14-a095ab7332be",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b82aacc6-86e1-4e44-9c5b-49d203973454",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _algorithm_.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be familiar with the word “algorithm” through their varied of prior experiences. Some students may have already taken a computer science course in which algorithms were defined. Other students may have heard it used in popular culture, as a reference to “the algorithm” that decides what they see online. Prompt a few students to reflect on where they’ve heard this word before and what it means to them."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b82aacc6-86e1-4e44-9c5b-49d203973454",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "261adbd1-f1ab-4f11-b592-b84fe1d65beb",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "261adbd1-f1ab-4f11-b592-b84fe1d65beb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7599627e-6cd5-43f2-abc1-ae3bdf41b85f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Get students started with considering how a computer could perform sentiment analysis. In their Activity Guide, they’ll come up with a general strategy a computer might be able to use. Later, they’ll turn it into pseudocode.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to Career Journeys: connections between lesson content and careers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the career students will explore in this lesson.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Exploring Sentiment Analysis"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7599627e-6cd5-43f2-abc1-ae3bdf41b85f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4b7a41e5-73a5-4b1c-9a7a-a7e03fe22fe9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we’ll explore the career of a market analyst: someone who uses data to help companies understand what people want and need. Market analysts use sentiment analysis regularly: it helps them understand how people are feeling about a topic.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4b7a41e5-73a5-4b1c-9a7a-a7e03fe22fe9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dbbd6362-cab1-4aa5-acd5-8ffc9855239b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Exploring Sentiment Analysis activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _strategy_.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _pseudocode_.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Describe an example problem to solve.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Share an example strategy for solving the problem.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Share example pseudocode that implements the strategy.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prepare students to start the Sentiment Analysis Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student pair a copy of the Sentiment Analysis Activity Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dbbd6362-cab1-4aa5-acd5-8ffc9855239b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b84cbe1-a690-4f19-8d43-ecdaa8fdd215",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Your challenge is this: given just the word lists on your Activity Guide, how could you tell whether a sentence like 'I had a wonderful day' is happy or sad? Don't worry about being a computer just yet—focus on brainstorming a strategy a human could follow step by step.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b84cbe1-a690-4f19-8d43-ecdaa8fdd215",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a9b91c71-56e8-418f-a011-68877bd30a05",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** While pairs brainstorm, look for groups that:\n- Are stuck on where to start. Prompt them with: \"What if you went through the sentence one word at a time? What would you check for each word?\"\n- Have already landed on a strategy. Push for depth. You could ask: \"What does your strategy do if a sentence has the same number of happy and sad words?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What strategy did you come up with? What are its strengths and weaknesses?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt pairs to share their strategies and learn from each other. Try to surface differences in approaches (e.g., counting happy vs. sad words, handling ties) so that students see there’s no single right answer. Look out for and validate observations about the limits of the word-list approach (e.g., that it may struggle with negation or sarcasm). End the discussion by pointing out the limitations students are finding are real limitations that motivate the AI techniques students will study later in the course."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a9b91c71-56e8-418f-a011-68877bd30a05",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "870006fe-8f6a-4ca4-8ec2-78006651899c",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We've thought about some interesting strategies. But to have a computer run these strategies, we need to write them down precisely. That's what we'll do next.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "870006fe-8f6a-4ca4-8ec2-78006651899c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bc611dd6-9b49-417a-8ec4-b5baa672d0f9",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students individually translate their brainstormed strategy into pseudocode, test it by tracing it on sample sentences, and then refine it with help from their partner. By the end, they have a written, tested, and revised algorithm they can explain and justify.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Writing Pseudocode"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bc611dd6-9b49-417a-8ec4-b5baa672d0f9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7d230c7a-35dd-4dcd-a4a6-ba6cc5107bf3",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Turn to the next page of your Activity Guide and try writing your strategy as pseudocode. When you're done, trace through it on the sample sentences: that is, follow your own instructions one step at a time and see if it gives you the right answer.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7d230c7a-35dd-4dcd-a4a6-ba6cc5107bf3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "40864c6c-8fd3-420c-89ba-794364109ece",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to the next page of the Sentiment Analysis activity guide. Have each student individually write their pseudocode and then trace it on the sample sentences before moving on.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** While students write and trace their pseudocode, watch for:\n- Students skipping steps that \"seem obvious.\" Encourage explicitness: \"How would someone reading this know to do X?\"\n- Students stuck on syntax. Remind them that pseudocode doesn't have to look like any specific language—clarity matters more than style.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to swap their Activity Guide with their partner, so that each partner can follow the other’s pseudocode.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Tell students to pay attention to how their partner follows their pseudocode, and to make any revisions based on seeing their partner do so."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "40864c6c-8fd3-420c-89ba-794364109ece",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b986a17-9b56-425b-a44f-c2c58eccdd2c",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students take a final pass at refining their algorithm, with the help of AI.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Refining Algorithms"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b986a17-9b56-425b-a44f-c2c58eccdd2c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "37a034a7-e979-4d7c-8a98-b4c952e6dd2d",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, let’s work individually to assess and refine your algorithm with the help of AI. You're in charge throughout—your job is to evaluate each suggestion the AI makes, rather than accept it automatically.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "37a034a7-e979-4d7c-8a98-b4c952e6dd2d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f8e0ac5-ab69-4753-891f-fa3490a91690",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Refining Your Algorithm"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f8e0ac5-ab69-4753-891f-fa3490a91690",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "40858d43-7646-45c8-9b36-eb24ed807a9c",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "40858d43-7646-45c8-9b36-eb24ed807a9c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1c25abb8-be54-4c70-a1c1-12097ee42662",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Levels 1–2.\n- **Level 1:** Students individually chat with AI to refine their algorithm.\n- **Level 2:** Students individually document their refined algorithm, describing what they changed and why.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the levels, watch for students accepting AI suggestions without questioning them. Push them to think: \"Why do you think the AI suggested that change? Does it actually improve your algorithm, or just make it more complicated? What new case did using AI surface that you hadn't considered?\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 2 to gauge whether students can engage critically with refining an algorithm. Look for students providing sufficient reasoning for incorporating or rejecting suggestions from the AI Chat level."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1c25abb8-be54-4c70-a1c1-12097ee42662",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2bd6ab62-2f90-445e-ae96-ba6f0f283162",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2bd6ab62-2f90-445e-ae96-ba6f0f283162",
        "lesson_activity.key": "17217f37-52f9-491a-910e-a55fd36628ad"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0b7e02b4-57f5-4898-8ff0-66c8cd433a2b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0b7e02b4-57f5-4898-8ff0-66c8cd433a2b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "17217f37-52f9-491a-910e-a55fd36628ad"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2f2fda32-ecca-44ad-8ff0-198ab4716baa",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Imagine you’re building a social media website. What’s the same on each profile page? What’s different?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students look at the three example of social media profiles and identify what they share in common and where they differ. The goal is to get students thinking about what information in a piece of software stays the same and what information changes, which will lead into students using variables in this lesson to represent data that might change. Students might identify that the “Message” and “Follow” buttons stay the same, or that each profile has a similar layout, or that the text colors and fonts are the same. Students might also identify that each profile differs in name, photo, and the specific numbers for the post and follower counts. If students only point out differences, prompt them to think about what patterns all the profiles share. Once students have shared a few examples of similarities and a few examples of differences, end the discussion."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2f2fda32-ecca-44ad-8ff0-198ab4716baa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "59354fcf-5c31-4346-861f-b22d6746f23d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3c97874b-52c3-4698-8c78-079867add0e3",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As we start writing code, some information in our code will always be the same, and some information in our code might change, just like how some information on these social media profiles is the same and some information changes.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3c97874b-52c3-4698-8c78-079867add0e3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "59354fcf-5c31-4346-861f-b22d6746f23d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9228353b-34b2-4768-9d6d-f149d02a78ab",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9228353b-34b2-4768-9d6d-f149d02a78ab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "59354fcf-5c31-4346-861f-b22d6746f23d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e136c9d2-428e-434b-8cf9-848fb224ed31",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students get experience using Python to print messages, accept input, and use variables.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Build a Chatbot"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e136c9d2-428e-434b-8cf9-848fb224ed31",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "879e0612-9a0a-440e-ad2b-fcec56dc5c04",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You might have used AI chatbots that can read and respond to your messages. Modern chatbots are powered by lots of complex logic and data. But today, we’re going to build a very simple chatbot that can start a conversation by greeting you.\n\nIn order to get the computer to do what we want, we need to give it instructions in a language the computer can understand.\n\nYou’ll be writing code in a programming language called Python to build programs that can behave intelligently.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "879e0612-9a0a-440e-ad2b-fcec56dc5c04",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "863e0dec-cddd-476e-8023-9a045bd0a574",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain to students that Python is a popular programming language used to build apps, websites, AI tools, and more.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This lesson also includes a [supplementary Python video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3uEANDnps3Y) that you can use in place of this section of the slides or as an additional resource for students."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "863e0dec-cddd-476e-8023-9a045bd0a574",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "49fe2d2b-b274-4cdd-82de-ca1412c4d806",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In Python, we can tell the program what output to produce by using print. If we run this program, it will print out the words “Good morning!” to the console.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students might need to be reminded that the console is a place where programs can provide text as output and can accept text as input."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "49fe2d2b-b274-4cdd-82de-ca1412c4d806",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "796b8d69-869c-4b2b-a61a-b00e19dc2fb3",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _function_."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "796b8d69-869c-4b2b-a61a-b00e19dc2fb3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8d681e6c-453a-4ca4-a8b1-bee85fa59ef5",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We can use functions to tell computers to do something. For example, the print function tells the computer to make some text appear in the console. When we “call” a function, that means we tell the computer to run the function.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8d681e6c-453a-4ca4-a8b1-bee85fa59ef5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e23da3e8-542e-40e5-a031-8ac4c90350f9",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain how to call a function. In Python, a function is called by using the function’s name and following it with parentheses.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _argument_."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e23da3e8-542e-40e5-a031-8ac4c90350f9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "18f005d2-19df-4b54-a7b1-c832d5733821",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Some functions take arguments as input so that they know what to do. For example, the `print` function takes an argument that tells it what message to print.\n\nBut we don’t always want our programs to print the same thing every time. Sometimes, we want programs that work with information that can change.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "18f005d2-19df-4b54-a7b1-c832d5733821",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d6c0d817-76b1-49b8-99e5-36422f0f263c",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _variable_.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain how variables can be assigned values in Python. The variable’s name goes on the left side of the `=` sign, and the value to store in the variable goes on the right.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that they as programmers can pick what names to give to their variables. It’s a good idea to give variables descriptive names so that it’s easier for people to read and understand what the code does."
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This lesson also includes a [supplementary Variables video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bqa3APR-Jqg) that you can use in place of this section of the slides or as an additional resource for students."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d6c0d817-76b1-49b8-99e5-36422f0f263c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d915e25d-5ff1-457a-bf37-92d1e7206d66",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _data type_.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that all values in Python have a data type. `int`, `string`, and `bool` are commonly used data types in Python, but there are others, too."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d915e25d-5ff1-457a-bf37-92d1e7206d66",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fdb41d43-a70e-4bef-b550-79eb271ffcd3",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Just like each profile had different names and post counts, variables let our programs store information that changes while the program itself stays the same.\n\nHere’s a Python program that defines a variable named `posts` to keep track of how many posts a user has on social media.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fdb41d43-a70e-4bef-b550-79eb271ffcd3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c95a1c28-6163-438b-b91e-93ba88482a26",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to suggest what they think the program will print, then reveal the answer.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Depending on how much time is available, you might consider having students briefly discuss in pairs what they think the programs on each of these next slides will print. Alternatively, you could have students vote on possible options. For example, on this slide, you might ask whether students think the program will print the number 31 or the word “posts”.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c95a1c28-6163-438b-b91e-93ba88482a26",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6953a85c-4a81-4f38-8000-7e3d33b62f93",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You can change the value that’s stored in a Python variable, too. To change the value in a variable, set it equal to something new.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6953a85c-4a81-4f38-8000-7e3d33b62f93",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "683fa400-b2dc-4dfa-8a02-1a58b472566b",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to suggest what they think the program will print, then reveal the answer."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "683fa400-b2dc-4dfa-8a02-1a58b472566b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8bdf347b-bfbd-42e1-a96b-9e41a3c71f6f",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If your variable is a number, you can also use Python for doing math with that number. You can use `+` for addition, `-` for subtraction, `*` for multiplication, and `/` for division.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8bdf347b-bfbd-42e1-a96b-9e41a3c71f6f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59724e55-8617-4b67-a8ca-2d6cb20824d8",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to suggest what they think the program will print, then reveal the answer.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59724e55-8617-4b67-a8ca-2d6cb20824d8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "14a4d2ea-8a41-4c7b-9d58-177eacb97349",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Sometimes, we want to include a variable inside text that gets printed. Here’s an example where the code tries to print out how many followers someone has.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "14a4d2ea-8a41-4c7b-9d58-177eacb97349",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5f3cab37-a6b9-4c37-a583-34ee7fc9880e",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to suggest what they think the program will print, then reveal the answer.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why didn’t this code print the number of followers?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students briefly discuss why the code didn’t work as expected. If students aren’t sure, prompt them with questions like “What did Python do with the word ‘followers’?” to help them identify that the word was treated as text to be printed rather than treated like a variable."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5f3cab37-a6b9-4c37-a583-34ee7fc9880e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "831012d7-c346-4065-bde1-b96ee18ad0dc",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This code didn’t work the way we wanted it to! The `print` function doesn’t know that we’re trying to print out the value stored inside of a variable.\n\nIn Python, we can use something called a formatted string to print text that includes variables in it.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "831012d7-c346-4065-bde1-b96ee18ad0dc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0e877cfd-b1bf-4264-b61d-1ce04799298a",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that a formatted string (or “f-string”) starts with the letter `f` before the first quotation mark. Then, variables can be named inside of curly braces to tell Python to include the contents of a variable in the string.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to suggest what they think the program will print, then reveal the answer.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0e877cfd-b1bf-4264-b61d-1ce04799298a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fab8057e-91e8-4ddd-844c-4e9f948927fc",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that you’ve practiced looking at some Python variables and functions, you’ll practice writing your own Python programs to tell a chatbot what to do.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fab8057e-91e8-4ddd-844c-4e9f948927fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "71d6b806-76d7-4fea-99e8-0b64ce735afc",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Build a Chatbot"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "71d6b806-76d7-4fea-99e8-0b64ce735afc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0bbfc3eb-6ccb-4fbc-b539-1fb0025db1ed",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1-3.\n- Level 1: Students print a message that greets the user.\n- Level 2: Students use a variable to store a user’s name and print a message that uses the variable.\n- Level 3: Students use variables of different types to print a message that includes today’s date.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the levels, check on how they are implementing each program. Students are likely to run into errors, so remind them that mistakes and debugging are normal parts of the programming process. Be on the lookout for several common types of errors students might run into when implementing their own functions:\n- Students might forget to put quotation marks around strings. Remind these students that Python uses quotation marks to tell apart code from text.\n- Students might struggle with printing variables inside a string. Remind these students that they can use a formatted string by adding the letter `f` before the quotation marks and surrounding the variable’s name in curly braces."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0bbfc3eb-6ccb-4fbc-b539-1fb0025db1ed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0cdaf860-752a-4764-9e19-83486ed255e8",
      "position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students write Python programs that accept input from the user.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Asking Questions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0cdaf860-752a-4764-9e19-83486ed255e8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ab654182-1fe0-47e1-8be6-929d16e73b79",
      "position": 25,
      "properties": {
        "description": "So far, we’ve been able to write Python programs that print output. But sometimes, we also want programs to accept input from the user, so the user can control what the program does.\n\nWe can use the `input` function for that. The input function prints a message but then lets the user type a response. It then gives us the response as a string that we can save inside a variable.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ab654182-1fe0-47e1-8be6-929d16e73b79",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fdfd9d18-e745-4063-9eca-d19d57320e70",
      "position": 26,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to suggest what they think the program will do, then reveal the answer.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This lesson also includes a [supplementary Accepting Input video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dpyoCSzVR3o) that you can use in place of this section of the slides or as an additional resource for students.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fdfd9d18-e745-4063-9eca-d19d57320e70",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "30bf185b-c1b0-4b18-90ec-33da6a9ec1ec",
      "position": 27,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The `input` function always gives us the user’s input as a string, which is text. But if we want to use the user’s input as a number instead, we can use a different function called `int` to convert the string into an integer.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "30bf185b-c1b0-4b18-90ec-33da6a9ec1ec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "29847213-3853-4a78-a8d9-4176539c468a",
      "position": 28,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to suggest what they think the program will do, then reveal the answer.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "29847213-3853-4a78-a8d9-4176539c468a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "da993460-71d2-46b7-9023-fb9a0647185e",
      "position": 29,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, let’s try writing some chatbot programs that ask the user for input, too.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "da993460-71d2-46b7-9023-fb9a0647185e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cd8fcc61-a6d2-41b5-b159-aadd1db1084d",
      "position": 30,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Asking Questions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cd8fcc61-a6d2-41b5-b159-aadd1db1084d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c1ebabfa-8fda-4ae9-8a16-4b994dce507a",
      "position": 31,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1ebabfa-8fda-4ae9-8a16-4b994dce507a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2c4933a4-5d90-4c96-889a-1eae0e6cff7e",
      "position": 32,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 4-6.\n- Level 4: Students prompt the user for their name and print a greeting with the name.\n- Level 5: Students fix bugs in a program that prompts the user for their age.\n- Level 6: Students complete a Check Your Understanding about reading a Python program that uses inputs and variables.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the levels, check on how they are implementing each chatbot. Students might struggle with the syntax of f-strings (Python formatted strings). Remind these students about the syntax for f-strings: using an `f` before the first quotation mark and surrounding variable names in curly braces. Students might also have trouble converting inputs to integers. For these students, show them how functions can be called inside of other functions, so that the `input` function can be inside of the call to the `int` function.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 6 as an assessment opportunity to evaluate how well students understand inputs, outputs, variables, and data types. If students are confused by particular functions, like the int function, that can be a good opportunity to discuss data types again and to remind students that every Python value has a data type. If students are confused about how the total value is calculated, revisit the concept of mathematical operators in Python, showing how to add, subtract, multiply, and divide values in Python programs."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2c4933a4-5d90-4c96-889a-1eae0e6cff7e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b475d6c-0b29-4aef-9731-2a07f78895d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6b967041-2fee-4bfa-a183-11ffeee5e9f8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students give specific examples from the code they wrote during today’s lesson.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6b967041-2fee-4bfa-a183-11ffeee5e9f8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bf31cf78-7621-4559-ae26-5be188699b78"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e4d86667-8d6f-4d3b-b2e2-6eab7d731bea",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e4d86667-8d6f-4d3b-b2e2-6eab7d731bea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bf31cf78-7621-4559-ae26-5be188699b78"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4dfbfa9f-d861-4b56-bbeb-5b7242841b8b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Invite students to look at these lines of code and describe what the code does.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What patterns do you see in this code?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify that the code consists of the same three steps repeated multiple times. In each instance, a city is defined, a temperature is defined, and an identically-formatted message is printed. If students aren’t sure how to think about patterns in code, ask them to look for lines of code that look similar to each other, or lines of code that are serving a similar purpose. End the discussion once students have identified some similarities across multiple lines of code."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4dfbfa9f-d861-4b56-bbeb-5b7242841b8b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b9fc5a45-6398-4c7c-904a-cf60a344bcf8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7ae397c0-caf2-4c1b-805e-61b68c12bebe",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When writing code, it can get repetitive if we have to keep writing the same lines every time we want the computer to do something similar. Today, we’ll be looking at strategies to make this easier.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7ae397c0-caf2-4c1b-805e-61b68c12bebe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b9fc5a45-6398-4c7c-904a-cf60a344bcf8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "17ca02e9-11c5-498b-aba4-d9b082270f57",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "17ca02e9-11c5-498b-aba4-d9b082270f57",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b9fc5a45-6398-4c7c-904a-cf60a344bcf8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "420cc2b3-7cfb-40b6-be59-d6bb26ebd25a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students learn to read function definitions and predict what their output will be.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _abstraction_. Explain that when building a complex software system, it’s helpful to split up some of the details so that you don’t have to think about all the details all the time.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Fitness Tracking Functions "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "420cc2b3-7cfb-40b6-be59-d6bb26ebd25a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5e290cf6-768b-4990-a3ac-67265b50ecc7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fbcb46d3-fce3-4a77-9ebe-d9a60e219a4e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "There’s lots of examples of technologies we interact with without knowing all the details. For example, when you get a snack from a vending machine, you might know how to press the buttons to get the snack you want. But you don’t need to know the details of how the vending machine parts move to give you your snack.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Abstraction can be a difficult topic for students to understand, so giving additional concrete examples can be helpful. For example, a microwave has complex internal mechanisms for heating up food. But those details are separate from the interface for using a microwave (entering a time and pressing a button). Separating the microwave’s button interface from its internal details makes it easy to use a microwave without needing to know exactly how it works."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fbcb46d3-fce3-4a77-9ebe-d9a60e219a4e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5e290cf6-768b-4990-a3ac-67265b50ecc7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7ab2e584-58a7-4245-bf81-20e09e8aea85",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Functions are examples of abstractions. You’ve used built-in functions like print and input to build programs, but you don’t need to know exactly what code is running inside of those functions. In this activity, you’ll learn to write your own functions to define code that you can reuse later.\n\nLet’s take a look at some examples of how functions are defined in Python so we can understand how to write our own functions too.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7ab2e584-58a7-4245-bf81-20e09e8aea85",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5e290cf6-768b-4990-a3ac-67265b50ecc7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "00eca889-8911-4fd5-b1a1-1ad058b7dc41",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Predict the Workouts"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "00eca889-8911-4fd5-b1a1-1ad058b7dc41",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5e290cf6-768b-4990-a3ac-67265b50ecc7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b87aa59e-9c1a-4946-b2c2-cdcc379f680c",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1-2.\n- Level 1: Students predict the output of a function.\n- Level 2: Students predict the output of a function that accepts a parameter.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the levels, keep an eye out for common challenges students might encounter when predicting.\n- Students haven’t yet been formally introduced to the syntax for defining functions and parameters, so they might be confused by syntax like the def keyword. You can tell them that this keyword is used to define what will happen when a function is called.\n- For students that are struggling with where to get started, encourage them to look for lines that include a call to print, drawing their attention to the likely form that the program’s output will take.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the example to show the steps for defining a function."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b87aa59e-9c1a-4946-b2c2-cdcc379f680c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5e290cf6-768b-4990-a3ac-67265b50ecc7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ff2417a5-e13c-402a-a7d8-6f89b23eebdb",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To create your own function in Python, we start with the keyword `def`—short for ‘define’. Then, we pick a name for our function that describes what it does.\n\nInside the function, we can write more Python code. All the code that’s part of a function needs to be indented. That’s how Python knows what code is part of the function.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students haven’t seen indentation in code before, explain that indentation is extra space added to the beginning of a line. Students can press the Tab key on their keyboard to indent a line. In Python, appropriate indentation is a required part of the language."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ff2417a5-e13c-402a-a7d8-6f89b23eebdb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5e290cf6-768b-4990-a3ac-67265b50ecc7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7899cc85-2ca3-4fb3-9ac6-df8f6acca54e",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Functions can also have variables that act as inputs to the function, like the `steps` variable here.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This lesson also includes a [supplementary Writing Functions video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mY0cNQcK7K4) that you can use in place of this section of the slides or as an additional resource for students.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7899cc85-2ca3-4fb3-9ac6-df8f6acca54e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5e290cf6-768b-4990-a3ac-67265b50ecc7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e4e9fcc4-da49-495c-bb4b-b2d98279fa85",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _parameter_.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the example function. Explain that `greet` has a parameter called `name`. `name` is a variable that can be used inside the function so that the function behaves differently depending on its input."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e4e9fcc4-da49-495c-bb4b-b2d98279fa85",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5e290cf6-768b-4990-a3ac-67265b50ecc7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7ca9c438-90f1-4105-9f27-1cf408aa34ca",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students define their own functions, including functions with parameters and with return values. In the process, students practice working with Python operators to manipulate numbers.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Define Functions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7ca9c438-90f1-4105-9f27-1cf408aa34ca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5e290cf6-768b-4990-a3ac-67265b50ecc7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b302806e-0429-4c44-aaff-fbbb1162f56e",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We’ve seen examples of functions that can accept input. Functions in Python can also produce output.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b302806e-0429-4c44-aaff-fbbb1162f56e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5e290cf6-768b-4990-a3ac-67265b50ecc7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e946aff9-ce85-4971-883c-4b50b727140b",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _return value_.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the example function. Explain that a function can produce output by using the word `return` followed by the output value. When a function returns a value, that value can then be used or saved in a variable.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through an example of defining and using a function that accepts input and produces output.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This lesson also includes a [supplementary Return Values video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qjKyZKRdHYA) that you can use in place of this section of the slides or as an additional resource for students."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e946aff9-ce85-4971-883c-4b50b727140b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5e290cf6-768b-4990-a3ac-67265b50ecc7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c67a83bc-731b-494d-a9fa-c0656ea20f2e",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This program defines a variable called age and prints its value in a message.\n\nThen, we use age as the input to the `next_number` function and save its return value in a new variable called `next_age`.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c67a83bc-731b-494d-a9fa-c0656ea20f2e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5e290cf6-768b-4990-a3ac-67265b50ecc7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f2439c16-ec0b-4e0b-81a2-37d0aee8b11a",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students what value they think is stored in the variable `next_age`. Help students see that the value should be `16`, the result of taking the `age` variable and adding one."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f2439c16-ec0b-4e0b-81a2-37d0aee8b11a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5e290cf6-768b-4990-a3ac-67265b50ecc7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "82c12cbb-0f6d-4923-96de-250804692cc8",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Since we saved the return value in a variable, we can use that variable in the rest of our program.\n\nNow, you’ll have a chance to write your own functions to define logic that programs can reuse.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "82c12cbb-0f6d-4923-96de-250804692cc8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5e290cf6-768b-4990-a3ac-67265b50ecc7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df8acc7b-7afe-4c02-a12c-42fad14f7efc",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Writing Functions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df8acc7b-7afe-4c02-a12c-42fad14f7efc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5e290cf6-768b-4990-a3ac-67265b50ecc7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1f183f58-7438-4c31-947b-8dbc2758ebae",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1f183f58-7438-4c31-947b-8dbc2758ebae",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5e290cf6-768b-4990-a3ac-67265b50ecc7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5dbb1fdd-1268-4475-aee5-14dd689d7272",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 3-7.\n- Level 3: Students write a function that prints a message.\n- Level 4: Students write a function that accepts input via a parameter.\n- Level 5: Students write a function that accepts multiple parameters.\n- Level 6: Students write a function that returns a value.\n- Level 7: Students complete a Check Your Understanding about reading a buggy Python program and identifying the problem.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the levels, keep an eye out for common errors students might make when writing their first functions.\n- Some students might not indent lines of code inside a function, or they might have inconsistent indentation. Remind students that Python requires code inside a function to be indented—you can prompt them with the question “Is this line a part of your function?” to help students decide whether the line needs to be indented. Help students with inconsistent indentation visually align lines of code that belong to the same function.\n- Some students might try to use parameter variables names outside of the function. Explain to students that since parameters are inputs to functions, those variables are only available inside the function itself (in the function’s “body”).\n- Some students might struggle with following the control flow of the program, especially when working with return values. Help walk these students through the steps that a computer would follow to run the code: point out when a function is being called, what values are provided as inputs, where to use those inputs, and where the return value can be saved in a variable. Students might also need to be reminded of the syntax for returning a value: the starter code already includes the return keyword, but students will need to provide the specific value that is produced as output to complete each return line.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 7 as an assessment opportunity to evaluate how well students understand functions, parameters, and arguments. If students have trouble identifying the bug, ask them to look at the function and identify how many inputs the function needs. Help students compare that to the number of values that are provided as arguments each time the function is called.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5dbb1fdd-1268-4475-aee5-14dd689d7272",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5e290cf6-768b-4990-a3ac-67265b50ecc7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "502ced24-4591-4230-baa3-afa0866bbc8b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "502ced24-4591-4230-baa3-afa0866bbc8b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "26671980-da68-4ada-b048-51640ac33faf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d18f9b65-0bca-4bd7-9f73-15d746c0eae6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d18f9b65-0bca-4bd7-9f73-15d746c0eae6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "06767262-2373-45e1-9399-cb3d82ff7170"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c9c56fd5-ab1f-4c9f-bd41-82574a87ae81",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Think of a decision you've made where the choice that seemed best in the moment turned out badly later. Or, a decision that seemed bad in the moment but turned out well. What happened?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students differentiate between short-term and long-term decision making. This discussion primes the Question of the Day. Strong responses describe a specific moment of divergence: for example, skipping homework to relax (feels good now, but bad later) or studying late for an exam (might feel bad now, but is good later). Weaker responses describe situations where \"best now\" and \"best later\" agreed: for example, \"I helped a friend, which felt good and was the right thing.\" If you get one of these, redirect with: \"Can you think of a decision where outcomes, now and later, pulled in opposite directions?\" End the discussion with: \"Hold onto these examples. We'll come back to a question like this once you've seen how it relates to decisions made by artificial intelligence.\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If you have a few extra minutes, ask students whether they think AI faces the same kind of decision. You don't need to land an answer: the hill-climbing algorithm they're about to learn about is itself a partial answer to this question."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c9c56fd5-ab1f-4c9f-bd41-82574a87ae81",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f89b1dff-a603-498c-bac3-430ea485cac4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "92f319eb-8ef1-4a3e-b652-ac1fe22ef380",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "92f319eb-8ef1-4a3e-b652-ac1fe22ef380",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f89b1dff-a603-498c-bac3-430ea485cac4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "80127a2e-46de-4e30-bef8-ac9a69638609",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the career students will explore in this lesson."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "80127a2e-46de-4e30-bef8-ac9a69638609",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f9c82575-72a9-4dc7-806f-9fb73e38b980",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we’ll explore the career of an operations researcher: someone who uses data and AI to find the best solutions to complex, real-world problems like improving hospitals, managing airline schedules, finding shipping routes, and more. You’ll get to think like an operations researcher in today’s activity.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f9c82575-72a9-4dc7-806f-9fb73e38b980",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b3729f67-ef8e-4da1-892f-942994cc14c3",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students take the perspective of an operations researcher, simulating how a smart thermostat could optimize the temperature in a room. They trace a simple \"always move toward the bigger neighbor\" algorithm by hand. The goal is to give students an embodied, intuitive grasp of optimization and hill climbing as they encounter these terms—and before they implement a similar algorithm in code in the second activity.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Smart Thermostat"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b3729f67-ef8e-4da1-892f-942994cc14c3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ca112704-70a1-4db9-9004-0233d6209670",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "For the next ten minutes, we're going to play the role of a smart thermostat. The thermostat's job is to find the temperature that the most people in this room prefer — and it has to do that without knowing anyone's preferences in advance.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ca112704-70a1-4db9-9004-0233d6209670",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7da61e95-d963-47ec-987d-f893eb6c3b43",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In AI, finding the best solution to a problem is called optimization, and it's one of the central things AI systems are designed to do.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7da61e95-d963-47ec-987d-f893eb6c3b43",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "311cc6fa-6a15-43c3-a3b4-cb7e73f046c8",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *optimization*. Connect the definition of optimization to the problem a smart thermostat faces."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "311cc6fa-6a15-43c3-a3b4-cb7e73f046c8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1ad52bee-f716-47b3-ad46-8cd8df945572",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Optimization is one of the central goals of artificial intelligence and of operations researchers. The problem of finding a temperature everyone prefers is an optimization problem: it’s about finding the best possible outcome given competing priorities or preferences.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1ad52bee-f716-47b3-ad46-8cd8df945572",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e8e4dce6-8612-467c-853e-fa729d7d47c7",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the instructions to explain how the Hill Climbing in the Classroom activity will unfold.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the instructions to explain how the Hill Climbing in the Classroom activity will unfold.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Smart Thermostat Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Collect class temperature preferences.\n- Read each temperature on the activity guide aloud, in order from lowest to highest.\n- For each one, ask: \"Raise your hand if you'd be comfortable, right now, with the room at this temperature.\" Ensure students know they aren’t picking their one “ideal” temperature—they’re raising their hand for each temperature at which they would feel comfortable.\n- Count the raised hands and announce the number. Have students draw a bar above that temperature on their activity guide whose height represents the number of people.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "To demonstrate how to complete this activity, consider drawing a graph on a whiteboard or chalkboard and filling it in as students raise their hands. Alternatively, you could edit the Activity Guide digitally and project your screen to students."
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "View the Smart Thermostat Activity Guide Exemplar to see an example of what a completed graph may look like."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e8e4dce6-8612-467c-853e-fa729d7d47c7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7d0de255-fe2c-4fab-83fd-02bc512c8aa3",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students simulate the thermostat.\n- Ask students to place a finger on any temperature on their activity guide—their choice.\n- Have them follow the algorithm written on the activity guide, moving their finger one step at a time, until the algorithm tells them to stop.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for which students reach the tallest bar and which stop somewhere lower. If the class data has more than one peak, some students will land on a smaller peak and stop there. Note which students did and didn't reach the global peak—this is expected and you'll use this in the next discussion.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students who finish their trace quickly can flip the activity guide over and sketch their own distribution. Challenge them to design one where the algorithm fails to reach the tallest bar, and then to identify the specific feature of their distribution that breaks the algorithm. This anticipates the local-maximum problem they'll meet in the levels."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7d0de255-fe2c-4fab-83fd-02bc512c8aa3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d1f0568f-7616-4c41-9a43-ef525d0392ac",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Reflect on your experience following the algorithm in the activity guide. How would you describe the way this algorithm works?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Debrief students’ experiences roleplaying the hill-climbing algorithm. Look for responses that touch on ideas like “The algorithm just decides whether to go right or left” or “Just by choosing between right and left, the algorithm ends up in a better place than before.” If students are quiet, consider prompting with simpler follow-up questions like: “While following the algorithm, what decision did you have to make?” Once 2–3 students have shared, move on."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d1f0568f-7616-4c41-9a43-ef525d0392ac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a365ba9-e548-4049-bc90-ad2ac69a1c0c",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, we’ll learn more about the kind of algorithm we just experienced first-hand.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a365ba9-e548-4049-bc90-ad2ac69a1c0c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f0801d48-6d0b-40e0-9dd8-4818cb2bfe6a",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *hill climbing*: a strategy that, at each step, moves in the direction of greatest “local” (nearby) improvement until no further improvement is possible. Connect it explicitly to what students just did with their fingers—they were running a hill-climbing algorithm by hand.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f0801d48-6d0b-40e0-9dd8-4818cb2bfe6a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a6a1586-296c-4d55-9037-f96c88e74edf",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "What you just did is a real algorithm that gets used in AI systems all the time. And the rule you followed is so simple that we can write it in just a few lines of Python code. That's where you're headed next.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "In the world of optimization, computer scientists and mathematicians distinguish between \"local\" and \"global\" optimums (also called \"optima\"). A local optimum is the best choice compared to its nearby alternatives—but not necessarily the best choice overall. A global optimum is the best choice of all. Hill-climbing algorithms make this distinction concrete. As the name suggests, these algorithms search the way a hiker might look for the highest point: repeatedly stepping toward whichever nearby option looks better. The strategy reliably reaches a local optimum (i.e., a spot where no neighbor is better) but it can leave you stranded on a small hill, unaware that a taller mountain sits across the valley. Hill-climbing is guaranteed to find a local optimum, but not the global one."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a6a1586-296c-4d55-9037-f96c88e74edf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "21f612e1-b7ec-4ed4-9a4b-7169e957d923",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students implement the hill-climbing algorithm in Python, using conditional statements to compare neighboring preferences and decide which way to turn the thermostat. They then encounter a preference distribution with two peaks, see the algorithm get stuck on the smaller one, and learn to call this kind of fast-but-approximate strategy a heuristic.",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Hill Climbing in Code"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "21f612e1-b7ec-4ed4-9a4b-7169e957d923",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a10ad69c-7388-457c-9f7b-2519f0005125",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Before you can write that algorithm in Python, your program needs one more tool: a way to ask a yes-or-no question and do something different depending on the answer. Let’s look at this tool now.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a10ad69c-7388-457c-9f7b-2519f0005125",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ebabcef-83cc-4896-8d5e-ee5b3c305da2",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A decision most everyone faces every day is what to wear. Let’s look at an algorithm about just that kind of decision-making. This one decides what clothes you should wear based on the temperature.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ebabcef-83cc-4896-8d5e-ee5b3c305da2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7217118c-9d1c-419a-bebf-8d74884c56c2",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to reading a flowchart diagram: start at the top, then follow the arrows down. State that each diamond represents a decision point.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to predict which clothes the algorithm will choose for various temperatures: 90℉, 70℉, and 60℉. For each temperature, walk through the flowchart node by node, verbalizing the decision-making process.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the algorithm’s implementation in Python."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7217118c-9d1c-419a-bebf-8d74884c56c2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "860e5dec-18f3-49e5-a7e6-9ed3af97f394",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s that same algorithm translated into Python code. We can use what we’ve learned about this algorithm to understand the Python syntax, step by step. We know that the algorithm first asks a question: is the temperature greater than 85 degrees? Line 1 of our Python code represents exactly this question. Let’s expand our vocabulary so we can talk more precisely about the code we’re seeing.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "860e5dec-18f3-49e5-a7e6-9ed3af97f394",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "10bee39d-6b6b-4c3c-b900-4dd950aca65f",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *Boolean expression*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *conditional statement*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Step through the conditional statement, explaining how the syntax maps to the algorithm."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "10bee39d-6b6b-4c3c-b900-4dd950aca65f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "53e93695-1713-4acb-9a28-0b42ff07910f",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "So, to be more precise with our vocabulary, line 1 is a conditional statement. Because it begins with if, you may also hear it called an if statement. It evaluates the Boolean expression temp > 85 and, if that expression is true, it will… print ‘T-shirt’—just like in our algorithm! Next comes another conditional statement—but this one looks a bit different: it uses the keyword elif. The keyword elif stands for “else if”—it comes after an if statement, and is a way to check another Boolean expression if the first one isn’t true. Now, if this Boolean expression is true… the program will print ‘Shirt’—just like we saw in our algorithm. Now, there’s another keyword: else. The else keyword is a way to say: if none of the prior Boolean expressions are true, do this. Notice that, if none of the prior Boolean expressions are true, our program will… print ‘Jacket’—again, just like in our algorithm! When writing conditional statements, elif and else are optional. You don’t always need them: if we just wanted to tell a user whether they should wear a t-shirt or not, we could simplify this conditional statement to just the first two lines of code.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "53e93695-1713-4acb-9a28-0b42ff07910f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "af92158a-e0a8-44d5-bf24-921e362fa982",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce Python’s comparison operators."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "af92158a-e0a8-44d5-bf24-921e362fa982",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6fce0c1e-e813-499a-a743-4d0de74c0e8d",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Before we continue, it’s worth knowing that it’s possible to do many different kinds of comparisons in a Boolean expression—not just the ‘greater than’ comparison we’ve seen here. The symbols between a and b are called comparison operators—they determine how a and b are compared.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6fce0c1e-e813-499a-a743-4d0de74c0e8d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "86ce300c-a121-4bd5-b48b-607100ed9974",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "description": "It’s time to apply what you’ve learned about hill climbing and conditionals to solve a problem like an operations researcher: programming a smart thermostat.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "86ce300c-a121-4bd5-b48b-607100ed9974",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "26c94f53-dd0f-4274-9e19-75b46431827c",
      "position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Hill Climbing in Code"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "26c94f53-dd0f-4274-9e19-75b46431827c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f7c52acc-2bc8-418e-af27-b4218f7d0b98",
      "position": 25,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f7c52acc-2bc8-418e-af27-b4218f7d0b98",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1235a5df-5c75-4c59-ad8c-41deeb15adbf",
      "position": 26,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 6.\n- **Level 1:** Students orient themselves to the smart thermostat program and identify the most preferred temperature.\n- **Level 2:** Students learn to control which way the thermostat turns.\n- **Level 3:** Students see how the `up`, `current`, and `left` parameters provide information about temperature preferences.\n- **Level 4:** Students make the thermostat behave intelligently, using conditionals to ensure it moves toward a temperature more people prefer.\n- **Level 5:** Students run a provided program against a temperature preference distribution with two peaks. They observe that the thermostat sometimes settles on the smaller peak.\n- **Level 6:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding, reflecting on whether the previous program always moves the thermostat to the best temperature.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** On Level 4, look for students who write only one if statement and miss either the left or right comparison—their thermostat will only turn one direction. Ask them: \"What does your program do when the better temperature is on the other side?\" On Level 5, look for surprise that theheir algorithm only finds a temperature more preferred than those around it, rather than the single most-preferred temperature: students may assume their program is broken when it isn't.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 6 as a formative assessment of whether students understand the trade-off they've encountered. If most students articulate that the program often finds a good answer but isn't guaranteed to find the best one, reinforce and move on. If responses suggest students think the program is simply broken, revisit Level 5 with them and ask: \"If you started your finger somewhere else on the activity guide, would you have ended up at the same peak?\" If responses are vague (\"it works sometimes\"), push for the mechanism: \"What about the algorithm causes it to stop too early?\""
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For students who finish Level 6 with extra time, pose an extension: \"How could you fix the way that your program only sometimes finds the best solution?\" Productive answers include \"try multiple starting points,\" \"sometimes move in a random direction,\" \"look two steps ahead instead of one.” Students don’t need to implement these ideas in code, just to consider whether there are solutions to the weaknesses they are encountering."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1235a5df-5c75-4c59-ad8c-41deeb15adbf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b98ae09-64be-4bcb-8920-b0c3d74aa422",
      "position": 27,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *heuristic*: a way to solve problems quickly but approximately. Tell students that the hill-climbing algorithm they just wrote is a heuristic: it doesn't always find the best temperature, but it finds a “good enough” one quickly. Heuristics are everywhere in artificial intelligence for exactly this reason."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b98ae09-64be-4bcb-8920-b0c3d74aa422",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "682eacaa-76e1-4552-8351-64e62c436156",
      "position": 28,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The algorithm you just wrote is something designers of artificial intelligence actually use. It’s not because it's perfect—you saw that it isn't. Instead, it’s because it's fast and usually good enough. The trade-off between perfection and speed is an important trade-off in artificial intelligence.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "682eacaa-76e1-4552-8351-64e62c436156",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e32e102-564c-48b9-b608-7542c19a4cb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "075ff746-0357-4799-968d-4bd0721a5634",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Have students share their responses with a partner or with the class. Connect responses back to hill climbing explicitly: hill climbing is a case where doing what looks best right now can sometimes—but not always—lead to what’s best later."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "075ff746-0357-4799-968d-4bd0721a5634",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b1c8d35-52a3-42d8-8c45-a7318a03b1e6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "24316ee2-80a7-48b4-9642-fd691e34b109",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "24316ee2-80a7-48b4-9642-fd691e34b109",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b1c8d35-52a3-42d8-8c45-a7318a03b1e6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a1d6bf5-dc0b-4332-b1fa-7c82ee2c3e44",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a1d6bf5-dc0b-4332-b1fa-7c82ee2c3e44",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8926653a-6476-48c2-bebe-48ba7f842307"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b97629f-c24d-47a8-974f-d37ad8adae45",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce the Ice Bucket Challenge as an example of a viral phenomenon created by continued sharing.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Ice Bucket Challenge. See if any of your students have heard of it or can explain it.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "The Ice Bucket Challenge"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b97629f-c24d-47a8-974f-d37ad8adae45",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ed675aad-1299-4d0a-8fc4-4cfffd4ce6c7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a3cdb136-a403-4a08-8e17-ac02482f23b2",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Has anyone heard of the Ice Bucket Challenge? In 2014, people shared millions of videos of themselves dumping ice water on their heads. The goal was to raise money to support research and care for those with ALS, a disease that affects bodily function. The Ice Bucket Challenge began with a single video, but quickly spread to millions.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a3cdb136-a403-4a08-8e17-ac02482f23b2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ed675aad-1299-4d0a-8fc4-4cfffd4ce6c7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cb6fcce2-19e0-4e04-a7ac-b1cbc0b94b94",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the “Jimmy Fallon Ice Bucket Challenge” video (0:00–3:40)",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Optionally use these timestamps to jump to key sections of the video:\n- 0:00–0:48 - Fallon introduces the Ice Bucket Challenge\n- 0:49–1:06 - Justin Timberlake challenges Fallon to take the Ice Bucket Challenge\n- 1:06–2:58 - Fallon and cast get ready to take the Ice Bucket Challenge\n- 2:58–3:30 - Fallon and cast take the Ice Bucket Challenge"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cb6fcce2-19e0-4e04-a7ac-b1cbc0b94b94",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ed675aad-1299-4d0a-8fc4-4cfffd4ce6c7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ee90676d-d4fb-4128-9a9a-1e156ad75401",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why do you think trends like the Ice Bucket Challenge become so popular, so quickly?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to consider how challenges, social media posts, and similar content might quickly become popular. Look for responses that touch on ideas like “People have to challenge someone else every time,” or “People get challenged by others, so they get brought into it.” These responses set up the premise of this lesson: that social media posts can go viral quickly if they’re shared with just a few friends, who then share it with their friends, and so on. Students may also bring up the idea of algorithmic sharing: that certain videos, because they’re popular, get recommended to more people. Treat this phenomenon as a kind of sharing too—one that amplifies the spread of a social media post. Once you’ve set up a foundation in the idea “sharing” or “reposting,” continue on."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ee90676d-d4fb-4128-9a9a-1e156ad75401",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ed675aad-1299-4d0a-8fc4-4cfffd4ce6c7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1ea53ad6-e16d-4880-aa1b-564ec05222ce",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Could anyone have predicted whether the Ice Bucket Challenge would become so popular? Today we’ll look at ways we can use computers to see how challenges, posts, and other things on the internet can spread so quickly—like the Ice Bucket Challenge.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1ea53ad6-e16d-4880-aa1b-564ec05222ce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ed675aad-1299-4d0a-8fc4-4cfffd4ce6c7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "47c1297a-a63b-4e8e-99d5-be79123e797f",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "47c1297a-a63b-4e8e-99d5-be79123e797f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ed675aad-1299-4d0a-8fc4-4cfffd4ce6c7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "76394af3-2273-46e8-a74f-e10dd54ac8b9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Help students build an intuitive model of how a social media post goes viral so they can later translate their model into code. By the end of the activity, students should recognize that the process by which posts go viral—repeated sharing—motivates a need for loops to model this process.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the theme of social media posts going viral. Ask students to call out posts, videos, or trends they’ve recently seen “blow up.”\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Going Viral activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *simulation*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Going Viral (Part 1) Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Share a worked example of the Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What’s the most important factor in a post going viral?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Debrief the Going Viral activity. Responses may suggest that the “share rate,” or the number of people each person shares the post with, is an important factor. Students may suggest that share rate is important because it determines how quickly the post spreads, or because it determines how its views compound. For example, students may notice that, every day, the share rate can double, triple, etc., the number of new views the post gets. Aim to guide students towards the realization that the share rate is important, but it’s not just the share rate: it’s also the fact that the sharing process is repeated day after day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use students’ experience repeating calculations in a simulation to transition into a motivated discussion of loops.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Going Viral"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "76394af3-2273-46e8-a74f-e10dd54ac8b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "80b36976-e283-46ce-8de7-b4000900ef3e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c185c7c3-7005-4f9a-80ac-1afdb87e9936",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students translate their hands-on simulation experience into Python code, learning to use for loops.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *loop*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *iteration*.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "For Loops",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For students who are more comfortable with programming vocabulary, consider disclaiming that iteration’s meaning changes based on context. For example, when speaking of iteration in general, the word’s meaning is closer to repetition."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c185c7c3-7005-4f9a-80ac-1afdb87e9936",
        "lesson_activity.key": "80b36976-e283-46ce-8de7-b4000900ef3e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a3cf8cb1-4541-4821-acc6-bc6e6b92f8b3",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *`for` loop*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Step through the example `for` loop."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a3cf8cb1-4541-4821-acc6-bc6e6b92f8b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "80b36976-e283-46ce-8de7-b4000900ef3e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b9c0373-6322-4a88-933d-27c7e79c9f55",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you know about for loops, it’s time to start your project! You’ll write a program to simulate a post going viral.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Going Viral in Code",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b9c0373-6322-4a88-933d-27c7e79c9f55",
        "lesson_activity.key": "80b36976-e283-46ce-8de7-b4000900ef3e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b251bbd1-0bc2-4340-8c6b-b8fba22c3afc",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Going Viral in Code"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b251bbd1-0bc2-4340-8c6b-b8fba22c3afc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "80b36976-e283-46ce-8de7-b4000900ef3e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f11088c8-2e41-491c-b778-6d61cdc9a2ab",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f11088c8-2e41-491c-b778-6d61cdc9a2ab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "80b36976-e283-46ce-8de7-b4000900ef3e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a174a910-fd6a-40af-a1e1-528c7db25f63",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 3.\n- **Level 1:** Students read a `simulate` function that's been \"unrolled\" (the same calculation written out three times) and predict what it prints.\n- **Level 2:** Students rewrite `simulate` using a `for` loop so it works for any number of days, not just three. They have a choice of continuing with the social media example, simulating an infectious disease, or simulating population growth.\n- **Level 3:** Students answer a free response question about when to use `for` loops.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, watch for students who finish quickly. Prompt them to experiment with different start, growth, and target values to see how the simulation behaves. Or, prompt them to choose another scenario in the Level 2 choice level."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a174a910-fd6a-40af-a1e1-528c7db25f63",
        "lesson_activity.key": "80b36976-e283-46ce-8de7-b4000900ef3e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a9fab881-298a-4a7e-a0a2-40b030289fa8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a9fab881-298a-4a7e-a0a2-40b030289fa8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7b9533d5-91f7-46d2-a7f9-1f22d7756efc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b4b071a-5e1f-4dfa-9355-b34b0637ccab",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b4b071a-5e1f-4dfa-9355-b34b0637ccab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4c8d89a3-7fe3-4c0b-bc03-5f6792c06e04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e9e811a2-33b6-4f8d-9ff6-5e0d4bbb9256",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *In 2022, Lionel Messi—a famous soccer player—made a post after winning the World Cup. How long do you think it took for the post to reach 10 million likes?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to think about virality in a different way: how quickly does a post reach a target number of views? Get students’ guesses from around the room, drumming up tension before the reveal.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If you’d like to add a little competition to the warm up, you could log students’ predictions on the board and play a game where the student whose guess is closest to the actual time—without going over—is the winner."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e9e811a2-33b6-4f8d-9ff6-5e0d4bbb9256",
        "lesson_activity.key": "55d8ee0b-99ea-4dbc-9c5b-26b2f938ec6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b4015926-7607-4123-b10d-57cb205e700c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal that the answer is 39 minutes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the theme of today’s lesson.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b4015926-7607-4123-b10d-57cb205e700c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "55d8ee0b-99ea-4dbc-9c5b-26b2f938ec6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b241fb3e-27f1-4724-a058-ba7f24ee7e84",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce students, by example, to a new form of simulation that continues until a goal is reached.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Time to 10 Million example.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Pause to take student input on how many minutes they think it will take for the post to reach 10 million likes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Step through the simulation.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point out the difference between the simulation students just saw and the ones they created in the previous lesson.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Time to 10 Million"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b241fb3e-27f1-4724-a058-ba7f24ee7e84",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6add7323-e5f1-4562-af85-da6814fd0186"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "42ea2f97-2cfe-4646-aca3-336109b0fbcc",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Teach students about while loops before they use them to implement a simulation.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *`while` loop*.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "While Loops"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "42ea2f97-2cfe-4646-aca3-336109b0fbcc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6add7323-e5f1-4562-af85-da6814fd0186"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fa67da51-a944-4cab-85fc-67039011e153",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A `while` loop is exactly the kind of loop that could be used to simulate a situation like the one we just saw. With a `while` loop, you can describe when the loop should keep repeating and when it should stop. In sum, use a `for` loop when you know how many times you'll repeat. Use a `while` loop when you repeat until a condition is met. Let’s look at a simple `while` loop to get our bearings.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fa67da51-a944-4cab-85fc-67039011e153",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6add7323-e5f1-4562-af85-da6814fd0186"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c2106168-5437-4891-9f58-d410343f9916",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the example while loop."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c2106168-5437-4891-9f58-d410343f9916",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6add7323-e5f1-4562-af85-da6814fd0186"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "14edf1be-eeb9-4170-a8e6-b361684ff5c5",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "`while` loops often use a condition involving the value of a variable. In this `while` loop, the condition is `i < 3`. So, we can expect this loop to iterate while `i` is less than three.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "14edf1be-eeb9-4170-a8e6-b361684ff5c5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6add7323-e5f1-4562-af85-da6814fd0186"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "da70fb70-2c0b-401b-9d89-e67ae5a919d2",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Step through the example while loop."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "da70fb70-2c0b-401b-9d89-e67ae5a919d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6add7323-e5f1-4562-af85-da6814fd0186"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "06ecdae1-5e4f-4c05-be9f-e4bef8002480",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students apply what they’ve learned about while loops by implementing a simulation of a post going viral.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Going Viral in Code"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "06ecdae1-5e4f-4c05-be9f-e4bef8002480",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6add7323-e5f1-4562-af85-da6814fd0186"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "90664874-d9bd-4133-bb64-bd3d9df1db53",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Going Viral in Code"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "90664874-d9bd-4133-bb64-bd3d9df1db53",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6add7323-e5f1-4562-af85-da6814fd0186"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "00b65665-d425-4830-90bf-1d79a3c97fe5",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "00b65665-d425-4830-90bf-1d79a3c97fe5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6add7323-e5f1-4562-af85-da6814fd0186"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "773d546f-7c50-4b2c-91d3-b20a7fb493bd",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 5.\n- **Level 1:** Students read a simulate function that's been \"unrolled\" (the same calculation written out three times) and predict what it prints.\n- **Level 2:** Students rewrite simulate using a `while` loop to implement one of three simulations of their choice.\n- **Level 3:** Students learn about logistic growth, a more realistic way to simulate growth.\n- **Level 4:** Students prompt AI to update their simulation so that it uses logistic growth.\n- **Level 5:** Students reflect on when to use `while` loops.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, watch for these scenarios:\n- **Level 2:** Students may write a condition that's off by one (e.g., `views <= target` instead of `views < target`) or forget to update views inside the loop, creating an infinite loop. If a student's program seems frozen, ask them to trace what changes (and doesn't change) inside the loop. You can also point students to the “Stop” button, which can end a program that’s stuck in an infinite loop.\n- Listen for students who finish quickly. Prompt them to experiment with different start, growth, and target values to see how the simulation behaves."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "773d546f-7c50-4b2c-91d3-b20a7fb493bd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6add7323-e5f1-4562-af85-da6814fd0186"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8011a161-d3d6-48cd-a492-149abcf9d7f6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
