      }
    },
    {
      "key": "63df12d3-df2c-43d5-969f-d77ba256b32c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students connect the role of a QA engineer to finding bugs in algorithms and code, exploring skills that QA engineers use to troubleshoot software.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _debugging_."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "63df12d3-df2c-43d5-969f-d77ba256b32c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8fa15db-d14f-42b0-93b3-febfcd0ac48c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0659290e-b556-4c93-ae39-c15b0d1a2621",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When you’re writing a program, it’s normal if the program doesn’t work perfectly the first time! Debugging is how we find problems in our programs and figure out how to fix them.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0659290e-b556-4c93-ae39-c15b0d1a2621",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8fa15db-d14f-42b0-93b3-febfcd0ac48c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f75ae2dc-e74d-4ca0-90a6-48e383382726",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Spotlight the role of a QA Engineer. Explain that QA engineers are responsible for testing software products and making sure they work. QA engineers can help find bugs in computer software and identify when and why those bugs happen.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the “Role of a QA engineer” video, which introduces students to a real QA engineer talking about their responsibilities and priorities.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the warm-up activity. Students will see the pseudocode for a buggy algorithm and will work with their partner to identify the bug."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f75ae2dc-e74d-4ca0-90a6-48e383382726",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8fa15db-d14f-42b0-93b3-febfcd0ac48c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "81526258-1ce2-459b-829d-17e0103a3f33",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In a moment, you’ll see the pseudocode for an algorithm for a music app. The algorithm should player the user’s favorite songs: playing all the 4-star and 5-star songs and skipping the 1-star, 2-star, and 3-star songs.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may need to be reminded that pseudocode is a way to plan out code using plain language. The structure of pseudocode can look like a programming language like Python, but is written to make it easier to focus on the logic rather than the language.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "81526258-1ce2-459b-829d-17e0103a3f33",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8fa15db-d14f-42b0-93b3-febfcd0ac48c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6e6fc7ff-27dd-4911-80db-ba6c33e5d912",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Help students find a partner.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to work with their partner to take the role of QA engineers by reading the pseudocode, discussing the logic, and identifying the bug.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students discuss, check in with pairs as they work through the pseudocode. For pairs that are having trouble finding the bug, suggest some debugging strategies:\n- Encourage students to read the code out loud.\n- Prompt students to trace the code one line at a time. For each line, they can consider what the computer will do with that line and whether that makes sense.\n- Have students imagine some sample inputs to the program (for example, a list of 5-star songs, or a list of 2-star songs). Ask them to run through the pseudocode and see how the program would behave for the sample input.\n- Ask students how they would have written the code so that they can evaluate whether their logic matches the pseudocode.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "You can also point students to the debugging strategies in their Unit Guide if they need help getting started with finding the bug.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6e6fc7ff-27dd-4911-80db-ba6c33e5d912",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8fa15db-d14f-42b0-93b3-febfcd0ac48c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "56251076-7e4e-4332-955e-053ef2606883",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Once most pairs have had a chance to discuss the pseudocode, invite one or more pairs to share with the class what bug they identified. Once the class has identified the bug (rating >= 4 or rating <= 5 will play all songs, not just the 4-star and 5-star songs), move on."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "56251076-7e4e-4332-955e-053ef2606883",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8fa15db-d14f-42b0-93b3-febfcd0ac48c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a28ca45c-d9af-4b01-9f09-7a025af643d1",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, we’ll be exploring strategies we can use to help find and fix bugs in our programs.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a28ca45c-d9af-4b01-9f09-7a025af643d1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8fa15db-d14f-42b0-93b3-febfcd0ac48c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fed58d8c-cef6-47c1-ac7e-e914a9c0ac6c",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fed58d8c-cef6-47c1-ac7e-e914a9c0ac6c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8fa15db-d14f-42b0-93b3-febfcd0ac48c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "373b25da-5016-4d82-96f7-e2206d088790",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students look at a program description and some sample input/output pairs and identify bugs. Through this activity, students learn to critically evaluate the results of running a program multiple times and to use those results to consider where possible bugs in programs might be.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Finding Bugs"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "373b25da-5016-4d82-96f7-e2206d088790",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ce34984-d7f0-4420-a951-34e32eaf6056"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1d0eae5e-4224-4fa3-abfc-2ec04a18a04f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In order to fix bugs in our programs, we have to find those bugs first! A great way to find bugs is by testing our code. We can run our code multiple times and see what happens.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1d0eae5e-4224-4fa3-abfc-2ec04a18a04f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ce34984-d7f0-4420-a951-34e32eaf6056"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c31c81e1-8cc1-411f-91a4-9e8973fc2005",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Make sure each student has a partner. You can re-use the partner groups from the warm-up activity or help students find new partners.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c31c81e1-8cc1-411f-91a4-9e8973fc2005",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ce34984-d7f0-4420-a951-34e32eaf6056"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "558715b1-4785-4327-b3d1-8a4f36114813",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this activity, you’ll see a description of three different programs. Then, you’ll see some inputs and outputs for that program, but the output won’t always match what the program is supposed to do. You and your partner should look at the inputs and outputs and discuss together what the bug in each program is.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "558715b1-4785-4327-b3d1-8a4f36114813",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ce34984-d7f0-4420-a951-34e32eaf6056"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9f2f9f9d-ae87-4831-8471-a22879b99620",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give each pair a copy of the Finding Bugs Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students work through the activity guide in pairs. The activity guide describes three different programs, each of which has been implemented with a logical bug. Students should evaluate the inputs and outputs to identify the mistakes and suggest where the bug might be.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Walk around as pairs work through the activity guide. If students are struggling, suggest strategies to help them first identify which outputs are incorrect. For example, you might have students read the program description and then predict what the outputs should be by looking only at the input column. Then, they can compare their predicted output to the actual output produced by the program. Once students have identified which outputs are wrong, encourage them to think about what kinds of inputs lead to the program making mistakes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What strategies are helpful for finding and fixing bugs?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify how they approached the process of debugging, with the goal of identifying general strategies that QA engineers and other software developers can use for debugging their programs. Strong responses will identify problem-solving techniques that can be used to identify errors, such as “we walked through the inputs one at a time” or “we tried to find patterns in the mistakes the program makes”. If students jump straight to describing their solution, follow up by asking them how they identified that problem. Once students have brainstormed a few strategies, you can move on.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to add to the debugging strategies list in their Unit Guide as they share their debugging strategies.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9f2f9f9d-ae87-4831-8471-a22879b99620",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ce34984-d7f0-4420-a951-34e32eaf6056"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7d099226-2bc5-42ce-b3da-2477b06cc63a",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students get practice debugging real Python programs by reading code, running code, reading error messages, and fixing errors in code. Through the process, students are also exposed to smart home devices as possible applications of programming logic.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the idea of “smart” devices in the home. Over time, companies that make thermostats, door locks, lightbulbs, vacuum cleaners, and other home products have been building more intelligent features into those products.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Debugging a Smart Home"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7d099226-2bc5-42ce-b3da-2477b06cc63a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ce34984-d7f0-4420-a951-34e32eaf6056"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f5d36b5-0386-429e-98da-ff3373cd5593",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In order for smart home devices to work, they need to be programmed to know what to do, and someone needs to verify that they work correctly. In this activity, we’ll act as QA engineers identifying and fixing bugs in these kinds of programs.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f5d36b5-0386-429e-98da-ff3373cd5593",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ce34984-d7f0-4420-a951-34e32eaf6056"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6cc0ae22-6c2e-425f-b12e-f7dc933ffc02",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Debug a Smart Home"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6cc0ae22-6c2e-425f-b12e-f7dc933ffc02",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ce34984-d7f0-4420-a951-34e32eaf6056"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "350f091e-e3a4-4012-bc39-3436515e45d1",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "350f091e-e3a4-4012-bc39-3436515e45d1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ce34984-d7f0-4420-a951-34e32eaf6056"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c48b3d0-1f70-49ef-94c3-9880cf865fa8",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 4.\n- Level 1: Students find and fix a syntax error in a smart speaker program.\n- Level 2: Students find and fix a type error in a robot vacuum program.\n- Level 3: Students find and fix a logical error in a smart lightbulb program.\n- Level 4: Students answer a free-response question that involves reading and understanding a Python error message.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** For each level, encourage students to start by running the program and seeing what happens. For students that are having trouble reading error messages, point them towards the parts of the error message where Python identifies the line number and the piece of code that is causing the problem. You can also encourage students to talk through the code with a classmate or talk to the AI Tutor to help them identify and fix bugs.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Each level focuses on a different type of error. Syntax errors are mistakes in words and symbols in the code that prevent the computer from understanding the program. Type errors are mistakes where the computer expects one type but gets another (like using an integer where a string should be). Logical errors are mistakes where the instructions defined by the program cause an incorrect result. Students don’t need to know the names for each of these types of errors, but consider introducing them if it helps students understand the error messages they see or how errors differ from one another."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 4 as formative assessment of whether students are able to extract relevant information from a Python-generated error. If students are having trouble interpreting the error message, prompt them with questions that help them identify relevant parts of the error message that can help with debugging. For example, you might ask “What’s the name of the variable that Python is complaining about?”, “What line of code is causing the problem?”, or “What does it mean for a variable to be defined?”\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c48b3d0-1f70-49ef-94c3-9880cf865fa8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ce34984-d7f0-4420-a951-34e32eaf6056"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c99be40e-fec5-4083-9080-7d590d8158b6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c99be40e-fec5-4083-9080-7d590d8158b6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6bcee4cd-4cb8-460e-afab-ef689f7e7abf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "01a3a0e1-e2ec-4834-b609-482151772a97",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "01a3a0e1-e2ec-4834-b609-482151772a97",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b9186e51-d714-4a29-aae0-d03752a54ea4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9398c076-8dff-420a-96aa-593c374485d4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce autocomplete."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9398c076-8dff-420a-96aa-593c374485d4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ef1f1b16-1ee0-4aaa-881f-50b2f42496e9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8bf5c980-041e-494d-91a2-05a86a73a22a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You've probably used autocomplete more times than you can count: every time your phone suggested the next word in a text or every time a search box guessed what you were looking for, you were using autocomplete. Autocomplete is everywhere, but most people never stop to ask: how does a computer know what I'm trying to type?",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Ask students to imagine using a phone or computer with autocomplete completely turned off. Students may recognize how much they rely on it."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8bf5c980-041e-494d-91a2-05a86a73a22a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ef1f1b16-1ee0-4aaa-881f-50b2f42496e9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a86bfcc-776e-4ca4-9652-aea1a7f8573b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How do you think a computer finds the words a user meant to type?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface students' ideas and intuitions about how autocomplete might work—there are no wrong answers yet. Listen for ideas that hint at the strategy students will explore: finding words that begin with what a user has already typed. Students may also raise ideas like \"the computer guesses what's most common\" or \"it learns from what you usually type.” These are considerations in today’s autocomplete systems, though they go beyond what students will explore in this lesson.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a86bfcc-776e-4ca4-9652-aea1a7f8573b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ef1f1b16-1ee0-4aaa-881f-50b2f42496e9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f9495948-f405-4ff6-b539-14695d6afa87",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students will learn about prefix matching—a rule autocomplete can follow to predict the words a user might mean to type.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Matching Words"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f9495948-f405-4ff6-b539-14695d6afa87",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5a9d16b0-4295-442f-9425-240f113ba753",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To decide which words to suggest, autocomplete needs a rule to follow.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5a9d16b0-4295-442f-9425-240f113ba753",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "84af0315-5bc9-4b9d-b140-309fefd656c6",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to prefix matching."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "84af0315-5bc9-4b9d-b140-309fefd656c6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a8d9d05c-bbc1-40ca-a029-d48e6ca977e0",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Autocomplete often finds words that begin with what a user has already typed. This is called prefix matching.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a8d9d05c-bbc1-40ca-a029-d48e6ca977e0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2c392d84-d3f0-4b61-a641-5033b8a04b30",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Suppose a user typed the letters “th.” What words could autocomplete suggest?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Check for students’ understanding of prefix matching. Prompt students to shout out examples, or call on students as they raise their hands. Valid words begin with “th.” Once students have named 3–4 valid words, you can move on."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2c392d84-d3f0-4b61-a641-5033b8a04b30",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "46268223-2a44-40cb-a3c4-9b1b2cdd1e0e",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you’ll write your own simple version of autocomplete in Python.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "46268223-2a44-40cb-a3c4-9b1b2cdd1e0e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cfe70676-b7a5-4e34-9be8-935d4a9e094e",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the program students will write."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cfe70676-b7a5-4e34-9be8-935d4a9e094e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8d0eebc0-53e6-4d67-860f-8b5692e42df1",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The program you’ll write will first ask you for a ‘fragment.’ A fragment is part of a word that a user has already typed, like ‘re.’ Next, the program prompts you for a prediction. This is the word the user may have meant to type. Let’s say you type in ‘read.’ If the prediction matches the fragment, the program will say ‘Could be!’ Otherwise, it will say ‘Probably not.’",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8d0eebc0-53e6-4d67-860f-8b5692e42df1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5a70d86d-c7cc-42b8-b5b6-2387979f8f1b",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students create a simple autocomplete program that uses prefix matching in Python.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Autocomplete in Code"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5a70d86d-c7cc-42b8-b5b6-2387979f8f1b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "79b0e7db-6607-4ccf-8031-4a3de5c09ddc",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Autocomplete in Code"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "79b0e7db-6607-4ccf-8031-4a3de5c09ddc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b54733d3-981c-47aa-884d-c3fdb758a00f",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 1 to begin Autocomplete in Code.\n- **Level 1:** Students explore their starter code and predict what it will do.\n- **Level 2:** Students use AI to learn how to implement prefix matching in Python."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b54733d3-981c-47aa-884d-c3fdb758a00f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "822c0f05-13af-400f-82d6-5cf29cc4ed9c",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Prompt students to see a limitation of their autocomplete program, motivating them to learn about objects and string methods in order to address the limitation.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to run their program and notice that it doesn’t consider \"Read\" to be a valid prediction when a user enters \"re.\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why do you think your program doesn’t treat \"Read\" as a valid prediction when a user types \"re\"?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to consider why a seemingly valid prediction isn’t valid according to their program. Look for students to suggest that it could be due to the strings’ mixed capitalization: the \"r\" in \"re\" is lowercase, while the \"r\" in \"Read\" is uppercase. If students don’t discuss capitalization, consider prompting students to look beyond the letters and to consider capitalization explicitly. Reinforce students’ observations about capitalization by revealing to them that Python compares strings case-sensitively: that is, uppercase and lowercase letters are treated differently.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Introducing Objects and Methods"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "822c0f05-13af-400f-82d6-5cf29cc4ed9c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a850b2e8-54e3-4b95-8b01-c64395a4548d",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To make Python compare strings case-insensitively, we need to learn about what strings actually are. A string is, in fact, something called an object.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This lesson also includes a [supplementary Objects video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Hto4hKhI21A) that you can use in place of this section of the slides or as an additional resource for students.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a850b2e8-54e3-4b95-8b01-c64395a4548d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c9d55b60-4d69-4ca8-9988-16f736135391",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *object*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c9d55b60-4d69-4ca8-9988-16f736135391",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "14968058-d891-415a-9efa-646bbd902e76",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In Python, all values—like strings—are really objects. This fact will come in handy today, as you’ll get to use some of the functions that come with strings. There’s actually a special name for the functions that belong to an object. Let’s see that next.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "14968058-d891-415a-9efa-646bbd902e76",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9fba4f19-be88-44d8-a142-94d2b2c14feb",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *method*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9fba4f19-be88-44d8-a142-94d2b2c14feb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6fa7627c-f1a0-41c3-9d7e-ef1bf0fc1801",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ll get to use string methods today: methods that operate on strings. Let’s look more closely at a few methods you might use.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6fa7627c-f1a0-41c3-9d7e-ef1bf0fc1801",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "88ee0d8c-8845-4fbb-ac43-6c3802c1416c",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to the `upper` method.\n- Point out that, in Python, programmers call methods via dot notation: that is, one writes the name of the object, followed by a dot, followed by the method one wishes to call.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to the `lower` method."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "88ee0d8c-8845-4fbb-ac43-6c3802c1416c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a4f0c19-ce8c-4940-b8bb-af73871484ca",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students improve their autocomplete program to find words case-insensitively.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Improving Autocomplete"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a4f0c19-ce8c-4940-b8bb-af73871484ca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ce3e4719-5fb3-4071-ada2-f3e95fedad97",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Improving Autocomplete"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ce3e4719-5fb3-4071-ada2-f3e95fedad97",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "737239d8-5c24-450e-ba5b-db4251594b1d",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "737239d8-5c24-450e-ba5b-db4251594b1d",
