      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c9c8d8db-29b3-4626-9e8a-7933ecc51de6",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 3.\n- **Level 3:** Students use AI to learn how to implement case-insensitive prefix matching in Python.\n- **Level 4:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding, answering the Question of the Day on the platform."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c9c8d8db-29b3-4626-9e8a-7933ecc51de6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5adfefa7-f239-4a4c-a8ce-e2543ec8f01a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "991c8a71-a9b2-4aac-9e65-f94c0cae7b78",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "991c8a71-a9b2-4aac-9e65-f94c0cae7b78",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3eca9b19-c721-466c-a132-d0e7d02ba7c6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "028215cd-8610-4c9c-9b38-024c073acc8e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "028215cd-8610-4c9c-9b38-024c073acc8e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3a5d5321-f5bf-4ccf-ac92-cc1f8599f3d0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c20a7d32-0e3e-4375-b83d-517f367d6e2b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students experience how a series of well-chosen yes-or-no questions can quickly narrow a large set of possibilities down to a single answer. This is the core idea behind a decision tree.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Pair students up and explain the rules of 10 Questions:\n- One partner thinks of an item from around the classroom.\n- The other partner asks up to 10 yes-or-no questions to figure out what the item is.\n- After one round, partners switch roles.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "10 Questions",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Shorten to 5 questions per round if time is tight. If students fall into pure guessing (\"Is it a pencil? Is it an eraser?\"), nudge them toward broader category questions like \"Is it bigger than a phone?\" or \"Is it made of metal?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c20a7d32-0e3e-4375-b83d-517f367d6e2b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "799aa3bd-240b-42c1-a618-cf82e10fa1e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0ae63c3f-1a7f-4841-b853-aedcd6bb770a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for the kinds of questions students are asking. Note pairs who ask category-splitting questions early (\"Is it something you write with?\")—you may want to call on them in the following discussion. If a pair is stuck only guessing, ask: \"What's a question whose answer would rule out a lot of items at once?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What kinds of questions worked best?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to reflect on questions that can quickly identify an unknown object from a large set of objects. Encourage students to recognize that broad, category-splitting questions (e.g., \"Is it metal?\") rule out many possibilities at once, while specific guesses (\"Is it the stapler?\") rule out only one item. A smart sequence of yes/no questions can reach a specific answer surprisingly fast. If students don't arrive there on their own, call on a pair you noticed asking strong questions and walk through their sequence. Or, follow up with another question: “Did some questions quickly narrow down possibilities? What were they?” End the discussion once the class has generally arrived at the insight that, in this context, some questions are better than others because they rule out many possibilities."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0ae63c3f-1a7f-4841-b853-aedcd6bb770a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "799aa3bd-240b-42c1-a618-cf82e10fa1e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fbab898e-c0b2-47c8-bc33-5bc6b30652ca",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The game you just played—asking yes-or-no questions to narrow things down to one answer—is exactly how something called a decision tree works. Decision trees use the same strategy you just used: to identify a snack, to sort an email, even to help a doctor narrow down a diagnosis. Today you'll learn how decision trees work, and then build some of your own in Python.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fbab898e-c0b2-47c8-bc33-5bc6b30652ca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "799aa3bd-240b-42c1-a618-cf82e10fa1e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "027379f2-fa62-45ba-910c-9cdb08452596",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "027379f2-fa62-45ba-910c-9cdb08452596",
        "lesson_activity.key": "799aa3bd-240b-42c1-a618-cf82e10fa1e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e3ba542a-d109-4390-b25d-be647818859c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Introduce the vocabulary of decision trees and walk the class through tracing an example tree as a group. By the end, students should be confident reading a decision tree. They'll apply this skill to code in the next activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the context this section’s examples will use.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Pose a question about the scenario to motivate the introduction of decision trees: \"Could AI do the same?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *decision tree*. Explain that a decision tree is like a visual version of the 10 Questions game: a diagram where each branching point is a question and the bottom of each branch is a final answer.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Tracing Decision Trees Together"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e3ba542a-d109-4390-b25d-be647818859c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20e54dae-b4da-4bd6-ab93-e791111fb7e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5e6f288f-8acb-412a-8514-ee90b9540927",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s look at a few examples of decision trees and learn how to read them.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This lesson also includes a [supplementary Decision Trees video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kyqRh7THEGY) that you can use in place of this section of the slides or as an additional resource for students.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5e6f288f-8acb-412a-8514-ee90b9540927",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20e54dae-b4da-4bd6-ab93-e791111fb7e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f2ec342a-94e3-48a1-bd28-a1ecdd39d8d4",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce an example decision tree."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f2ec342a-94e3-48a1-bd28-a1ecdd39d8d4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20e54dae-b4da-4bd6-ab93-e791111fb7e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a16fbd7-4710-4a99-ad77-b9434f3b7011",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This decision tree helps a person know what to do when they feel sick. Let’s see how it works by looking at multiple people.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a16fbd7-4710-4a99-ad77-b9434f3b7011",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20e54dae-b4da-4bd6-ab93-e791111fb7e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ced196b-8d0a-4834-a7e4-5832952917d3",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Trace the tree together by calling on students at each step:\n- “What question does the tree ask first?”\n- “Based on our scenario, which branch do we follow?”\n- “Now what does the tree ask?”\n- “Where do we end up?”\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Repeat with a different example."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ced196b-8d0a-4834-a7e4-5832952917d3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20e54dae-b4da-4bd6-ab93-e791111fb7e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5d6d901f-745e-44b4-babe-705d904f1706",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Move from tracing decision trees on slides to building them in Python using nested conditional statements. Students predict what a tree does then implement one step by step.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Decision Trees in Code"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5d6d901f-745e-44b4-babe-705d904f1706",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20e54dae-b4da-4bd6-ab93-e791111fb7e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6917d157-b4d3-4a86-862c-c7d2d165184d",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We just traced decision trees together. Now you'll see what the same structure can look like in code. The questions in a decision tree are really just conditional statements.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6917d157-b4d3-4a86-862c-c7d2d165184d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20e54dae-b4da-4bd6-ab93-e791111fb7e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bbcb49ed-ed2a-401d-bc72-4146aa015875",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Illustrate how the previous decision tree’s logic is now implemented via conditional statements in Python. Emphasize how to read nested conditionals."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bbcb49ed-ed2a-401d-bc72-4146aa015875",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20e54dae-b4da-4bd6-ab93-e791111fb7e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "20611278-979a-40b9-b68c-4ed20632a2af",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Decision Trees in Code"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "20611278-979a-40b9-b68c-4ed20632a2af",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20e54dae-b4da-4bd6-ab93-e791111fb7e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8c20aac1-77b0-4837-8bc3-761b5d23a388",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8c20aac1-77b0-4837-8bc3-761b5d23a388",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20e54dae-b4da-4bd6-ab93-e791111fb7e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7a247298-5593-48c0-96af-a7fa8b5c3b46",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 4.\n- Level 1: Students read a decision tree written in Python and predict the decision it will make for a given input.\n- Level 2: Students choose between two contexts, implementing a decision tree that asks one question.\n- Level 3: Students extend the previous decision tree to ask two questions.\n- Level 4: Students complete a Check Your Understanding that asks them to describe a decision tree in words.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who:\n- Get tangled in nested conditional statements. Walk them through the indentation as a tree shape. Consider having them write comments to make the structure clearer.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For students who finish early, challenge them to extend their Level 3 tree with a third, fourth, or fifth level of nesting."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7a247298-5593-48c0-96af-a7fa8b5c3b46",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20e54dae-b4da-4bd6-ab93-e791111fb7e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "56bbd949-da95-4165-bb18-f3e95e2bd756",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Now that you’ve seen decision trees, what kinds of problems do you think they can solve?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to imagine the kinds of problems that could be solved by decision trees. Strong answers will identify problems that suit a decision tree’s structure: generally, problems that have known \"inputs” and that can be solved by asking a series of questions about those inputs. For instance, the problem of figuring out what to wear in the morning is a good example of a problem a decision tree could solve: the problem’s inputs (e.g., the weather forecast, where you’ll be that day) are known, and it’s possible to ask questions about those inputs to arrive at a conclusion. Weaker answers may identify problems that suit only a decision tree’s goals: generally, any problem for which it’s helpful to reach a “decision.” Encourage students to propose specific problems. As they propose these problems, reason with them about whether the structure a decision tree offers is truly a good fit for the problem: is it, in fact, possible to know the problem’s inputs and to ask questions about those inputs that would lead to a good decision? Once the class has identified a few problems, continue with the Wrap Up.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "56bbd949-da95-4165-bb18-f3e95e2bd756",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7d937d15-1457-4a58-bf4a-f5d15f00a31b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "190da890-3229-4328-9d2f-2af3635a924a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "190da890-3229-4328-9d2f-2af3635a924a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1e292e44-7fbb-42f2-8778-3c9f722296f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "31176b1e-ea67-441d-8b41-ae76de4a1a04",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Think back to the kinds of AI systems you've built in this unit: a simple chatbot, a smart thermostat, a decision tree, and more. What do they all have in common?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Activate students’ memories of what they’ve built this unit and help them recognize that every AI system they've built so far has been driven by explicit rules that they—the human—wrote in advance. Listen for responses like \"we had to write the rules,\" \"they use if-then statements,\" or \"we told them what to do.\" If students focus only on what the systems can do (e.g., \"they all respond to inputs\"), redirect by asking, \"How does each system know how to respond?\" Use this to set up the definition on the next slide.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Give students 30–60 seconds for a quick pair-share before opening the floor. This helps reluctant participants formulate ideas and gets more voices in the room."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "31176b1e-ea67-441d-8b41-ae76de4a1a04",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a844e32b-9e4b-4e1d-beae-719320b9f082"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5bd56d74-ecac-4784-af4a-f70ef56a60d9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students about the definition of logic-based AI."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5bd56d74-ecac-4784-af4a-f70ef56a60d9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a844e32b-9e4b-4e1d-beae-719320b9f082"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cbddebc1-d3cd-4abc-a60f-ab2364315562",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "All the AI systems you've built in this unit are examples of logic-based AI. They work because we, as humans, can state the rules they need to follow up front—and we can always read those rules back later to understand exactly what the system is doing. Today, we're going to look at where this approach starts to break down.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cbddebc1-d3cd-4abc-a60f-ab2364315562",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a844e32b-9e4b-4e1d-beae-719320b9f082"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "74c77eb2-aaaa-433e-9518-fd2a5abea54b",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "74c77eb2-aaaa-433e-9518-fd2a5abea54b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a844e32b-9e4b-4e1d-beae-719320b9f082"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7412a8f1-cd82-4762-8c38-66119b6875fc",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students discover that some of the things they \"just know\"—like whether a face belongs to a human or a robot—are nearly impossible to fully express as rules. This experience sets up the idea of *tacit knowledge* as a fundamental limit on logic-based AI.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Tacit Knowledge"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7412a8f1-cd82-4762-8c38-66119b6875fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b5af6789-4320-419f-a4cb-95fbebea336a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Imagine we want to build an AI system that decides whether a picture shows a real person or a robot. We're going to try to build it together and see how hard that turns out to be.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b5af6789-4320-419f-a4cb-95fbebea336a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a4dc27b7-6cf3-42b4-91c9-8a2aab97ff0e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Tell students they will see three pairs of images. In each pair, one is a photograph of a robot. Have students get ready to vote on which is the robot."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a4dc27b7-6cf3-42b4-91c9-8a2aab97ff0e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0abbf697-5e25-4836-8c99-e9b166bd78af",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As you share your observations, focus on evidence you see in the image. Avoid comments that judge or make assumptions about a person's appearance, identity, or characteristics.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Be explicit that these are real photographs of real robots, not AI-generated images. The task is to spot the robot, not to spot a fake image."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0abbf697-5e25-4836-8c99-e9b166bd78af",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "efb5c987-f6c1-4806-a591-a67728a7fdbe",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students vote with their hands."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "efb5c987-f6c1-4806-a591-a67728a7fdbe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3ab4f7eb-0e5b-47bb-b7c6-31121e04d002",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Raise your hand if you think the left image is the robot. Raise your hand if you think the right image is the robot.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3ab4f7eb-0e5b-47bb-b7c6-31121e04d002",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "99e2b1af-6163-4ab8-ab58-c2f2f67feacd",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Confirm the correct answer. Then, call on one student and ask: \"How did you know?\" Keep the discussion of \"how did you know\" brief here—just one student response per slide. The richer conversation comes later, once students have seen all three pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Repeat the hand vote.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Confirm the correct answer. Then, call on one student and ask: \"How did you know?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Repeat the hand vote one more time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Confirm the correct answer. Then, call on one student and ask: \"How did you know?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show all answers at once. Point out that students (likely) identified all three robots quickly and with high confidence.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** It’s pretty easy to tell which images contain robots. Now, let's try the hard part: imagine I'm a computer, and I can only follow rules you give me. What rules could I use to tell a robot from a human? The rules have to work on any robot and any human—not just these three.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students productively struggle to create rules that are both clearly stated and universally applicable. Typical responses may include:\n\n- \"The eyes look weird\"\n  - Ask students: \"What kind of rule would help a computer tell weird eyes from normal eyes?\"\n- \"The skin looks fake\"\n  - Ask students: \"How would a computer measure 'fake-looking' skin?\"\n- \"They look stiff\"\n  - Ask students: \"Could you define a clear rule that distinguishes ‘stiff’ from ‘not stiff?’\"\n\nThe point of the discussion is not to find a good rule. Rather, it's to see how hard it is to do so. After 2–3 minutes, transition by saying: \"Notice how hard it is to explicitly state a good rule. You all knew the answers quickly, but you can't fully explain how.\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *tacit knowledge*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "99e2b1af-6163-4ab8-ab58-c2f2f67feacd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "14122f0b-6592-43ac-b1af-16410cd8777d",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "What you experienced has a name: ‘tacit knowledge.’ It's knowledge you have but cannot fully put into words. You knew which face was the robot, but you couldn't write down a rule that would always work. Logic-based AI struggles with anything that depends on tacit knowledge, because we can only build it from rules we can state.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "14122f0b-6592-43ac-b1af-16410cd8777d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f24e47fe-612c-474c-a743-6555ef85a128",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students complete the takeaway sentence in their Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f24e47fe-612c-474c-a743-6555ef85a128",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6e2f49ae-ac69-4fbd-abdf-c5b2f2b357ef",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students see that even when a problem can be solved with rules, the number of rules needed can explode beyond what's practical to write. This sets up the concept of *combinatorial explosion*.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Many Possibilities"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6e2f49ae-ac69-4fbd-abdf-c5b2f2b357ef",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a366a447-fb9e-4e6d-84c1-672fd153e048",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let's learn about a new limitation of logic-based AI. This time, we're going to build a chatbot that answers questions about a school library. Unlike the last problem, this one seems like it could be solvable with rules—but let's try.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a366a447-fb9e-4e6d-84c1-672fd153e048",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a9e51cac-cb5d-4dd5-bc89-d73be4d0fdc9",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students the empty code editor. Tell them you're going to build a library chatbot together, one rule at a time, and they're going to help find the holes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the first version of the code. Point out that the chatbot is now configured to only answer questions that contain the word \"library.\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the updated code. Now the chatbot can answer questions like \"What time does the library close?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** In 30 seconds, can you stump this chatbot? Turn to a partner and come up with a question it would fail on.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Get students thinking about limitations by poking holes in the current rules. Have 2–3 pairs share their stumpers (e.g., \"When is the library closing?\" \"Is the library closed?\" \"What time does it open?\" \"Can I check out a book?\"). Capture a few on the board—you'll address some on the next slides and leave others as unsolved problems. The goal is to surface how many different ways a single intent can be phrased."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a9e51cac-cb5d-4dd5-bc89-d73be4d0fdc9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "23be950f-ea54-4775-b230-4e3778b22abe",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Suppose someone asked ‘When is the library closing?’ How would the chatbot handle that question? We’d need to explicitly ask a question about whether ‘closing’ is in the user’s question.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "23be950f-ea54-4775-b230-4e3778b22abe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6cbfbd7a-be8f-4951-a50d-d6dcaafa6401",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the new code. Now the chatbot handles both \"close\" and \"closing.\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Did this fix everything? What other phrasings would still slip through?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that there are many more ways to ask the same question: \"shutting down,\" \"still open,\" \"open until,\" etc. The possible variations on phrasing alone are endless, and we haven't even started adding new topics yet.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the updated code. Now there's a holiday rule.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What if a student asks: \"Is the library open on the 4th of July?\"*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should notice that the word \"holiday\" isn't in that question, so the chatbot would miss it entirely. This motivates the next slide, where we have to list every holiday by name.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the latest code. Highlight that we now need to list every holiday by name, and that there are dozens.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** In 60 seconds with a partner, list as many additional rules as you can think of that this chatbot would still need. Don't worry about writing the code—just the topics.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students see just how insurmountable a task it is to name all the rules that would need to be coded to create a working chatbot. Have pairs quickly tally how many they came up with, then have the class shout out totals. Encourage coming up with many examples: \"Where is the library?\", \"How long can I check out a book?\", \"Can I bring food?\", \"Is there a quiet room?\", \"Do you have [specific book]?\", \"Where is the bathroom?\" Tally a rough class total on the board.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *combinatorial explosion*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6cbfbd7a-be8f-4951-a50d-d6dcaafa6401",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0c3b0b4f-778d-4d15-a4e4-5b8b4af63512",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "What we’ve noticed has a name. When a problem has so many possibilities that you'd need an unmanageable number of rules to cover them, we call it a combinatorial explosion. The number of rules grows so fast that no team of humans could ever finish writing them.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0c3b0b4f-778d-4d15-a4e4-5b8b4af63512",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e190bb1d-a963-4d3a-b519-217a5db13c73",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students complete the takeaway in their Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e190bb1d-a963-4d3a-b519-217a5db13c73",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ab34dce3-ce37-4677-ac43-d6156b58d325",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students see that even when a rule-based system works well on the inputs it was designed for, it can fail on unexpected ones. This motivates the concept of *brittleness*.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Unexpected Inputs"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ab34dce3-ce37-4677-ac43-d6156b58d325",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fef445e3-e71b-4cf4-ac34-5d754bd9b217",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We have one more problem to try. This time, we're going to use a decision tree to decide how ripe a banana is.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fef445e3-e71b-4cf4-ac34-5d754bd9b217",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "16a36cca-c23d-4ca6-a738-51c47e3d0922",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the decision tree so students know the rules.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the green bananas. Have students pair up and trace the decision tree together. After each pair is ready, have the class call out their answers together.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the brown bananas. Have the same pairs trace the decision tree together. After each pair is ready, have the class call out their answers together.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the yellow bananas. Once again, have the same pairs trace the decision tree together. After each pair is ready, have the class call out their answers together.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the spotted bananas. Before tracing, ask: \"What do you predict the tree will say? Let’s vote: ripe, overripe, or underripe?\" Then trace together.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Are there large spots on this banana? Or just normal spots?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to see some ambiguity in the decision tree. Quickly get students’ opinions. The goal isn’t to arrive at a right answer, but instead to see that there may be disagreement about the kinds of spots that are on these bananas.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the half-green, half-yellow banana. Before tracing, ask again: \"What do you predict the tree will say? Let’s vote: ripe, overripe, or underripe?\" Then trace together.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Is this banana green? Or yellow?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students again to see some ambiguity in the decision tree. Quickly get students’ opinions. The goal isn’t to arrive at a right answer, but instead to see that there may be disagreement about the color of the banana.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students predict the results, then trace as a class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Green? Yes! So it's . . . an underripe BANANA?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Humorously identify a limitation of the decision tree: this apple isn’t a banana. However, the decision tree has no way to recognize the difference between apples and bananas: it will confidently declare an apple \"underripe,\" because the tree only knows the rules given to it."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "16a36cca-c23d-4ca6-a738-51c47e3d0922",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3ee3417a-841a-48a1-851d-375d7dbc0cec",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Our tree confidently calls a Granny Smith apple an underripe banana. It has no way to say \"I'm not sure\" or \"this isn't a banana.\" It just follows the rules.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3ee3417a-841a-48a1-851d-375d7dbc0cec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "340d4df6-3ed7-4ab4-a746-48ebfc401a6f",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *brittleness*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "340d4df6-3ed7-4ab4-a746-48ebfc401a6f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4eabc592-50ca-4f00-a7d8-584288d3092f",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This tendency to fail when the input doesn't fit the rules is called brittleness. A brittle system works great on the inputs it was designed for, but it doesn’t work the moment something unexpected comes along.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4eabc592-50ca-4f00-a7d8-584288d3092f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7585f553-7c24-43a0-bc74-d50d22367785",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students complete the takeaway in their Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7585f553-7c24-43a0-bc74-d50d22367785",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "926516c0-e69e-49fe-a134-47ced9710566",
      "position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "926516c0-e69e-49fe-a134-47ced9710566",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ede18473-c729-40c5-ab8a-d4e5812ebd6d",
      "position": 25,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 12, Level 1. Students answer the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ede18473-c729-40c5-ab8a-d4e5812ebd6d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6d5166b-2917-4b98-ab6c-ea6ce578eda3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe9d8aae-0ef2-465f-97a6-5628f78b4f57",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *You’ve seen three limitations of logic-based AI systems. What solutions can you imagine?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Tee up the introduction of machine learning in the next unit. There are no right or wrong answers in this discussion: listen and respond to students’ ideas, while guiding the discussion towards broad approaches that are representative of machine learning (e.g., “maybe a machine could learn the rules itself, so we don’t have to write them”). If students are hesitant to propose solutions, consider emphasizing the imagine part of this discussion: they don’t need to propose a working solution immediately—they just need to think about what might need to happen for AI do some of the tasks they’ve seen well. Once you’ve guided the discussion towards topics related to machine learning, you can move on."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fe9d8aae-0ef2-465f-97a6-5628f78b4f57",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2cb30305-f289-46b9-aaab-71bb7fe6f3c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eac6044f-a365-4584-8ebf-c150c8eb98f0",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To solve the kinds of problems we’ve seen today, we need to go beyond logic-based AI. We need a kind of AI that can learn, rather than being explicitly programmed. In the next unit, we’ll explore just that topic: *machine learning*.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eac6044f-a365-4584-8ebf-c150c8eb98f0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2cb30305-f289-46b9-aaab-71bb7fe6f3c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "28c56b33-6277-40be-8634-46b1e5c2f8a2",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "28c56b33-6277-40be-8634-46b1e5c2f8a2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2cb30305-f289-46b9-aaab-71bb7fe6f3c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0f441771-eb16-413b-8044-8b6644a09c0d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0f441771-eb16-413b-8044-8b6644a09c0d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "44f3e717-7d19-4a78-a625-41ffb17b4ebe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "51222e68-7312-4146-b105-2fd3811847d2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Imagine a new student at school wants your advice about what classes to take. What questions would you ask them first to help you come up with good advice?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should suggest questions that uncover information about a student that might indicate what classes they would be interested in. Some questions might be about the student’s prior course experiences (e.g. “What was your favorite class last year?”, “What math classes have you taken already?”) and other questions might be about the student’s interests (e.g. “Do you like history?”, “What do you want to do after high school?”, “What do you like to do for fun?”). If students struggle to think of questions, you can prompt them to think about why a class might be a better fit for one student than another. Once students have suggested a variety of different questions, end the discussion.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Warm-Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "To give students more opportunities to brainstorm possibilities, consider having students talk to each other in pairs before students share ideas in the whole-class discussion.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "51222e68-7312-4146-b105-2fd3811847d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0006894-a330-45ac-9ddc-30795bd9e7c5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3fb5e4d9-007f-4541-b745-f07b6eb4da29",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When helping someone make a decision, it’s often useful to have a conversation with them first, asking questions that can help make a better decision.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3fb5e4d9-007f-4541-b745-f07b6eb4da29",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0006894-a330-45ac-9ddc-30795bd9e7c5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "613a6ef7-d1e0-41af-93a7-777f28c17cab",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "613a6ef7-d1e0-41af-93a7-777f28c17cab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0006894-a330-45ac-9ddc-30795bd9e7c5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f9fe29f6-55b9-4486-9531-626a49a66cd5",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students start brainstorming possible real-world use cases for conversational AI that are of personal interest to them. They begin to plan the specific decisions that their conversational AI might suggest.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _conversational AI_.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students for examples of conversational AI they might already know about or use.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Brainstorming Conversational AI"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f9fe29f6-55b9-4486-9531-626a49a66cd5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0006894-a330-45ac-9ddc-30795bd9e7c5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "20ce9734-23a3-45de-882e-5abfbfbd6dff",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A common way that people interact with AI is through conversations. People can talk back and forth with AI, and AI can provide answers based on what it hears. For the next few days, you’ll be implementing your own conversational AI that can help users make a decision.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "20ce9734-23a3-45de-882e-5abfbfbd6dff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0006894-a330-45ac-9ddc-30795bd9e7c5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "05631d69-d536-4cc2-b4d1-fd54444d0b10",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give some examples of types of decisions that an AI could help with by asking questions of a user. For example, a conversational AI might help a user decide where to eat, what movies to watch or books to read, or what to do this weekend. Tell students that they can decide what kind of decision they want their AI to help with."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "05631d69-d536-4cc2-b4d1-fd54444d0b10",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0006894-a330-45ac-9ddc-30795bd9e7c5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a112cd5a-7b01-461a-aec0-22ab29b259a5",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Modern conversational AI can understand open-ended language; today, you’ll build a rule-based version that keeps it simple with yes/no questions.\n\nThe project you’ll create will ask the user questions. Based on the answers to those questions, your program will ask more follow-up questions, and eventually suggest a decision.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a112cd5a-7b01-461a-aec0-22ab29b259a5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0006894-a330-45ac-9ddc-30795bd9e7c5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d3864bcd-2377-4a63-b4a3-266e1a30c2cb",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the example decision tree on the slide. Explain that the decision tree branches based on the answer to the AI’s question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point out that the decision tree shown on the slide branches just once, but decision trees can branch multiple times by asking follow-up questions. For example, if a user says they like music, a follow-up question might be “Do you like to sing?”\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Hand out the Conversational AI Project Planning Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete Step 1 of the Project Planning Guide. Students will brainstorm possible topics that their conversational AI could help with and brainstorm a list of specific decisions the AI could suggest.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the activity, check in with students about their brainstorming process. For students who need help brainstorming, encourage them to think about what they know a lot about and topics for which they would be able to provide good advice. As students brainstorm specific decisions, encourage students not to worry yet about what particular questions will lead to their AI recommending those decisions: for now, the focus is on generating ideas first.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "To help students generate ideas, encourage them to share their brainstorming with their neighbors and to suggest ideas to one another.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d3864bcd-2377-4a63-b4a3-266e1a30c2cb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0006894-a330-45ac-9ddc-30795bd9e7c5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2b4da61d-e998-4be5-be69-3dc49f5826cf",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Brainstorming Questions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2b4da61d-e998-4be5-be69-3dc49f5826cf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0006894-a330-45ac-9ddc-30795bd9e7c5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "41b96b57-26bd-4fae-83e2-5cbe8915e38b",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 13, Level 1 use AI Chat to brainstorm more decisions for their conversational AI.\n- Level 1: Students brainstorm more possible decisions for their conversational AI.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look and listen for the following:\n- Students who don't know what to ask the AI Chat. Help students write a prompt that describes their idea for their project and any possible decisions they’ve already brainstormed in Step 1 of their Project Planning Guide. As students read the responses from AI Chat, encourage them to think about which ones make sense for their project, and have students add any new ideas they like to their Project Planning Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "41b96b57-26bd-4fae-83e2-5cbe8915e38b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0006894-a330-45ac-9ddc-30795bd9e7c5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cadc5953-afd6-4c01-a930-59dc1423f703",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students will draw a first draft of their decision tree, planning the questions and answers that will be asked to help lead the conversational AI to make a decision.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the project requirements and project rubric.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete Step 2 of the Project Planning Guide, where they will draft the logic their conversational AI will follow by drawing a decision tree.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check in with students as they draw their decision trees.\n- Students can represent the decision tree however they’d like, as long as it’s visually clear how questions branch into other questions or decisions. For students looking for how to get started, encourage them to write a first question at the top of the tree and draw lines representing each possible answer.\n- Remind students that this is just a draft! They’ll have opportunity to revise and iterate on their decision tree throughout the project.\n- Some students might struggle to come up with questions to ask. In that case, you can point students back to their list of specific decisions they brainstormed in Step 1 and ask them to think about how they’d categorize those suggestions into groups. For example, if a student is creating a conversational AI to recommend weekend activities, they might notice that some of their decisions are “outdoor” activities and others are “indoor” activities, which could be then be turned into a question (“Do you prefer outdoor activities?”)\n- For students who are concerned, let them know that not all conversations need to have the same number of questions. For example, it’s okay if one path through the decision tree only involves two questions while another path involves three.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Draft a Decision Tree",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to use pencil, so that they can erase and revise parts of their decision tree as they iterate on their design.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cadc5953-afd6-4c01-a930-59dc1423f703",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0006894-a330-45ac-9ddc-30795bd9e7c5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "78540ded-15a8-49aa-82c2-9c4e99e5d3ce",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Tell students to complete the Day 1 Reflection section in their Project Planning Guide.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap-Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "78540ded-15a8-49aa-82c2-9c4e99e5d3ce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0006894-a330-45ac-9ddc-30795bd9e7c5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e894c444-6e17-45f0-97ec-01dd7dbc642d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to pull out their Project Planning Guide from Day 1.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students take turns sharing their decision tree diagram with their partner. Partners should make suggestions for each other’s decision trees.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students share their decision trees with each other, ask them to offer suggestions to help their partner iterate on their decision tree. If students struggle to provide feedback, prompt them with questions like “What other decisions could you imagine the AI making?” or “What other questions might be useful to help the AI make a good decision?”",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Warm-Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e894c444-6e17-45f0-97ec-01dd7dbc642d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f7440683-65cb-4029-b22a-503064c720da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "411cb054-e3cc-4340-85ec-e3b058861500",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students revise their decision tree by iterating on their own logic and responding to feedback provided by their partner during the warm-up activity.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Revise Your Decision Tree"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "411cb054-e3cc-4340-85ec-e3b058861500",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f7440683-65cb-4029-b22a-503064c720da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9ff92a15-1e81-4ff7-a630-f2d2e193daf1",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Before we start writing code, it’s helpful to think through the logic first. As you look at the draft of your decision tree, think about how you might improve it. Do you want to add or remove any decisions? Do you want to change any questions or change the order in which they’re asked? Now, you’ll have a chance to revise your decision tree based on your own ideas and your partner’s feedback.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9ff92a15-1e81-4ff7-a630-f2d2e193daf1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f7440683-65cb-4029-b22a-503064c720da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9b6eacb3-c7eb-4406-b6c4-0958296ffa62",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students complete Step 3 in their Project Planning Guide. Students will revise their decision trees by thinking about their partner’s feedback and by considering how they want their conversational AI to behave.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check in on students as they revise their decision trees. For students who aren’t sure what to change, ask them whether there are other questions they could ask to help make a good decision. You can also encourage students to check their logic for their decision tree to make sure that each decision matches the questions that led to that decision. If any decision is in the wrong place, students might move that decision to somewhere else in the tree."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9b6eacb3-c7eb-4406-b6c4-0958296ffa62",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f7440683-65cb-4029-b22a-503064c720da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "be36414e-3d74-4de2-a660-1eb479981d1c",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students begin translating their logical ideas for their decision tree into Python code, using functions, variables, and conditional statements.\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Start Building Conversational AI"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "be36414e-3d74-4de2-a660-1eb479981d1c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f7440683-65cb-4029-b22a-503064c720da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7a2e1284-4617-4221-893f-5558c40a8718",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In a moment, you’re going to take the decision tree you’ve designed and turn it into a Python program that you can talk to. You’ll use if statements to let your program branch in different directions, just like the decision tree you drew branched in different directions based on answers to questions.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7a2e1284-4617-4221-893f-5558c40a8718",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f7440683-65cb-4029-b22a-503064c720da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a29f7ad-8603-4f3d-8bed-c7a85d8d2194",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Build Conversational AI"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a29f7ad-8603-4f3d-8bed-c7a85d8d2194",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f7440683-65cb-4029-b22a-503064c720da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e8bec806-1d72-42f1-8f0d-b597a66299b7",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the code example on the slide. In this activity, students will have access to a pre-written function called `ask`, which asks a question to the user and prompts them for a yes/no answer. Explain that the function will also check to make sure that the user’s response is valid: if it’s not valid, the user will be prompted to try again.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that the `ask` function returns `True` if the user types “yes” and `False` if the user types “no”. The output of the function can then be used in an if statement to decide what logic to run.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 13, Level 2 to begin implementation of the Conversational AI Project.\n- Level 2: Students practice using the ask function in preparation for building their own conversational AI.\n- Level 3: Students implement their decision tree as a Python program to build conversational AI.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students that they’ll have opportunity the next day to work on this program, so they don’t need to finish it today.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look and listen for the following:\n- Students who don't know where to start. Point them towards their decision tree (e.g. by asking “What’s the first question you should ask the user in your program?”) Encourage students to implement and test their code one step at a time, rather than trying to build the entire program first and then run it.\n- Students who aren’t sure how to ask follow-up questions. Remind students that if statements can be nested inside of other if statements, as long as each additional block of code is indented more. Some students might struggle with the nested indentation, so help those students identify what lines of code should only run if a particular condition is true or false, and then help those students indent those lines appropriately.\n- Students who finish early. Encourage students to test their program by running it a few times and providing different answers. Students can continue iterating on their program so that it can ask more questions and make better decisions.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 2 as an opportunity to assess students’ comfort with function calls, storing using return values from functions, and using conditional logic. If there are parts of Level 2 that students struggle with, help students walk through the code step by step so that they can identify the role of each part of the program, helping to prepare students to write that logic themselves in Level 3.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be curious about the implementation of the ask function. Students don’t need to know how the function itself is implemented, but they’re welcome to explore it if they’re interested!\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e8bec806-1d72-42f1-8f0d-b597a66299b7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f7440683-65cb-4029-b22a-503064c720da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ef36f9f1-e152-4bb5-bf78-79b586a68153",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Tell students to complete the Day 2 Reflection section in their Project Planning Guide.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap-Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ef36f9f1-e152-4bb5-bf78-79b586a68153",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f7440683-65cb-4029-b22a-503064c720da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aea14358-20b2-4307-b84f-07e863746e7d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Take a look at this code. There are two bugs that will prevent this program from working. What bugs do you see?",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Warm-Up",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aea14358-20b2-4307-b84f-07e863746e7d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b028c1a-7a4e-4962-bb25-4c6b3646490e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6afc7a32-28e9-4885-be67-f09b3a91c0de",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students discuss with their neighbors what bugs they see, then invite students to share answers with the class for what bugs they see and how to fix them. There are two bugs: missing quotation marks around the string in the argument to ask, and extra indentation of the else block.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6afc7a32-28e9-4885-be67-f09b3a91c0de",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b028c1a-7a4e-4962-bb25-4c6b3646490e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0caafee5-0ef9-4cc8-9a99-5a1319fcf07f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Remember, it’s normal to run into bugs in your code, especially as we’re starting to write longer programs. It’s often helpful to test your code as you go, so that you can catch bugs earlier in the development process.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0caafee5-0ef9-4cc8-9a99-5a1319fcf07f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b028c1a-7a4e-4962-bb25-4c6b3646490e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f367e2ea-c014-4155-953d-d402b0905c6e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students complete the implementation of their conversational AI projects in Python.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 13, Level 3 to continue implementation of the Conversational AI Project.\n- Level 3: Students implement their decision tree as a Python program to build conversational AI.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look and listen for the following:\n- Students who don't know where to start. Point them towards their decision tree (e.g. by asking “What’s the first question you should ask the user in your program?”) Encourage students to implement and test their code one step at a time, rather than trying to build the entire program first and then run it.\n- Students who aren’t sure how to ask follow-up questions. Remind students that if statements can be nested inside of other if statements, as long as each additional block of code is indented more. Some students might struggle with the nested indentation, so help those students identify what lines of code should only run if a particular condition is true or false, and then help those students indent those lines appropriately.\n- Students who finish early. Encourage students to test their program by running it a few times and providing different answers. Students can continue iterating on their program so that it can ask more questions and make better decisions. Students can also help a classmate if they finish early.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students try each other’s conversational AI projects and provide feedback. Students can comment on what worked well and on what suggestions they might have for possible future additions.",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Build Conversational AI"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f367e2ea-c014-4155-953d-d402b0905c6e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b028c1a-7a4e-4962-bb25-4c6b3646490e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a94f82ea-c20a-43c6-948c-f66c2c714a8e",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap-Up",
        "progression_name": "End-of-Project Reflection"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
